  "contracts": {
    "lem-qft-tensor-test-function-density": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, with $n\\ge1$. A complex-valued function is smooth if its real and imaginary parts are $C^k$ for every finite $k$, in the sense of [[def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables]]. Coordinate derivatives act on these two parts separately. We write $\\partial^\\alpha$ for the multi-index convention there, and $\\partial^0\\varphi=\\varphi$.\n\nThe **test-function space** is $\\mathcal D(\\Omega)=C_c^\\infty(\\Omega;\\mathbb C)$: its members are smooth functions whose support, the closure in $\\Omega$ of their nonzero set, is a compact subset of $\\Omega$. Equivalently, the zero extension to $\\mathbb R^n$ is smooth with compact support contained in $\\Omega$: compactness gives a neighborhood of every boundary point disjoint from the support, where that extension vanishes. Functions are actual functions, not equivalence classes.\n\nPointwise addition and scalar multiplication make this a complex vector space; the support of a sum lies in the union of the two compact supports. The zero function has empty support. If $\\Omega=\\varnothing$ there is just the empty function, which is the zero vector. The topology is specified in subsequent definitions. Distribution pairings throughout this page are complex bilinear, with no conjugation on test functions. One may restrict all constructions to real scalars.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-test-function-topology",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open. For the fixed-support spaces of [[def-fixed-support-test-function-frechet-space]], let $\\mathcal P$ be the set of all seminorms $q:\\mathcal D(\\Omega)\\to[0,\\infty)$ such that $q|_{\\mathcal D_K}$ is continuous for every compact $K\\subseteq\\Omega$. A seminorm means $q(av)=|a|q(v)$ and $q(v+w)\\le q(v)+q(w)$.\n\nThe **test-function topology** is the topology generated by translated finite intersections of sets $\\{\\varphi:q(\\varphi)<\\varepsilon\\}$, where $q\\in\\mathcal P$ and $\\varepsilon>0$. Equivalently it is the finest locally convex topology making every inclusion $\\mathcal D_K\\hookrightarrow\\mathcal D(\\Omega)$ continuous; the universal-property lemma below proves this equivalence and the topology assertions. This is the locally convex inductive-limit or **LF topology**, not the unrestricted final topology of arbitrary spaces.\n\nFor precision, a locally convex topology here is a vector topology generated by a family of seminorms, and boundedness means absorption by every zero-neighborhood: for each such neighborhood $U$ there is $t>0$ with $B\\subseteq tU$. Finite intersections may be empty, giving the whole space. When $\\Omega$ is empty there is only the zero vector and its unique topology. No convergence criterion for sequences is used in the definition; that criterion will be a theorem.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-sequential-convergence-in-test-function-space",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For tests $\\varphi_j,\\varphi\\in\\mathcal D(\\Omega)$, $\\varphi_j\\to\\varphi$ in the LF topology if and only if the supports of $\\varphi_j$ eventually lie in one compact $K\\subseteq\\Omega$ and $\\partial^\\alpha\\varphi_j\\to\\partial^\\alpha\\varphi$ uniformly on $\\Omega$ for every multi-index $\\alpha$. Equivalently one compact contains all their supports and that of the limit, and convergence holds in its fixed-support topology. This is a sequence criterion, in ZF.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $m\\ge1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be open, and let $f:U\\to\\mathbb R$. A **multi-index** is $\\alpha=(\\alpha_0,\\ldots,\\alpha_{m-1})\\in\\mathbb N^m$. Put\n\n$$|\\alpha|:=\\sum_{i<m}\\alpha_i,\\qquad \\alpha!:=\\prod_{i<m}\\alpha_i!,\\qquad h^\\alpha:=\\prod_{i<m}h_i^{\\alpha_i}\\quad(h\\in\\mathbb R^m).$$\n\nHere $|\\alpha|$ and $\\alpha!$ use the natural-number sum and product of [[def-nat-finite-sum-and-product]], and $n!$ is the factorial of [[def-factorial-and-falling-factorial]]. By contrast, $h^\\alpha$ is the finite product in $\\mathbb R$ of [[def-finite-sum]], with the natural exponents interpreted by [[def-integer-power]]. For the zero multi-index $0$, set $D^0f:=f$. For nonzero $\\alpha$, write\n\n$$D^\\alpha f:=\\partial_0^{\\alpha_0}\\cdots\\partial_{m-1}^{\\alpha_{m-1}}f$$\n\nfor this displayed, canonical order whenever it exists. Coordinate partial derivatives have the meaning fixed in [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]].\n\nFor $k\\in\\mathbb N$, $f$ is of class $C^k$ on $U$ when, for every word $(i_1,\\ldots,i_r)$ of coordinate indices with $0\\le r\\le k$, the iterated derivative $\\partial_{i_r}\\cdots\\partial_{i_1}f$ exists and is continuous on $U$; the word of length $0$ denotes $f$. Thus this definition does **not** presuppose that differently ordered derivatives are equal. Equality of their values is a later theorem under these regularity hypotheses.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-test-function-cutoffs-and-euclidean-localization",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In ZF, for compact $K\\subseteq\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ with $\\Omega$ open, there is $\\chi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\Omega)$ with $0\\le\\chi\\le1$ and $\\chi=1$ on a neighborhood of $K$. Every open cover of $\\Omega$ admits an at most countable locally finite smooth partition of unity with compact supports, each support contained in some cover member. Subordination here asserts existence of such a member for each support; it does not select cover labels.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "cor-compact-domain-maps-are-uniformly-continuous",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Every continuous map from a nonempty compact Hausdorff space to a uniform space is uniformly continuous.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-fejer-uniform-convergence-for-continuous-periodic-functions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb C$ be one-periodic and continuous. Then\n\n$$\\sup_{x\\in\\mathbb R}|\\sigma_Nf(x)-f(x)|\\longrightarrow0 \\qquad (N\\to\\infty).$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-complex-exponential",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\iota_{\\mathbb R}\\colon\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb R$ be the canonical-natural map\nof [[def-canonical-natural]], and let $j\\colon\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb C$ be the real\nembedding $j(x)=x+i0$ from [[def-complex-numbers-and-arithmetic]]. For every\n$n\\in\\mathbb N$, the factorial $n!$ is nonzero by\n[[def-factorial-and-falling-factorial]], so\n$\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!)>0$ by [[lem-of-naturals-positive]] and consequently\n$j(\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!))\\ne0$ in the complex field\n[[thm-complex-numbers-form-a-field]].\n\nFor $z\\in\\mathbb C$, define\n$$ \\exp z:=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{z^n}{j(\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!))}. $$\nwhenever this complex series converges. Inside a complex expression we\nabbreviate the embedded denominator $j(\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!))$ by $n!$, so the\nsame definition may be written $\\exp z=\\sum z^n/n!$ without identifying a\nnatural number with a complex number. Powers and series are those of\n[[def-complex-integer-powers]] and\n[[def-complex-series-power-series-and-absolute-convergence]]. The convergence\nfor every $z\\in\\mathbb C$ is discharged by\n[[lem-complex-exponential-series-converges-everywhere]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-complex-exponential-is-entire-with-derivative-itself",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The complex exponential is entire, and\n\n$$ \\exp'(z)=\\exp z $$\n\nfor every $z\\in\\mathbb C$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-complex-exponential-addition-and-real-extension",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For all $z,w\\in\\mathbb C$, $\\exp(z+w)=\\exp z\\exp w$. For real $x$, the complex value $\\exp(x+0i)$ equals the published real exponential $e^x$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[def-complex-exponential]], [[lem-complex-exponential-series-converges-everywhere]], [[lem-cauchy-product-of-absolutely-convergent-complex-series]], [[def-real-exponential-function-and-e]], [[lem-binomial-theorem-over-complex-numbers]], [[thm-binomial-closed-formula]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For real $x,y$, $\\exp(x+iy)=e^x(\\cos y+i\\sin y)$ and $|\\exp(x+iy)|=e^x$. In particular $e^{i\\pi}+1=0$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[thm-complex-exponential-addition-and-real-extension]], [[thm-eulers-formula]], [[lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws]], [[thm-pythagorean-and-parity-identities-for-all-six-trigonometric-functions]], [[cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity]], [[thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas]], [[def-pi-via-first-positive-cosine-zero]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-continuous-on-a-rectangle-is-riemann-integrable",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Every continuous real function on a closed nondegenerate rectangle $Q\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$, $m\\ge1$, is Riemann integrable.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A\\subseteq\\mathbb R^p$ and $B\\subseteq\\mathbb R^q$ be nondegenerate closed rectangles, and let $f:A\\times B\\to\\mathbb R$ be Riemann integrable. Then the four lower and upper section-integral functions of [[def-sections-and-iterated-riemann-integrals]] are Riemann integrable and\n$$\\int_A\\ell_B=\\int_Au_B=\\int_{A\\times B}f=\\int_B\\ell_A=\\int_Bu_A.$$\n\nIf the $B$-sections are integrable outside a content-zero set $N\\subseteq A$, every bounded exceptionally completed function $h$ with $h(x)=\\int_Bf_x$ for $x\\notin N$ is integrable and\n$$\\int_{A\\times B}f=\\int_Ah.$$\nThe same assertion holds with the coordinate blocks exchanged. In particular, when every section in an order is integrable, the ordinary iterated integral in that order exists and equals the multiple integral. The theorem does not assert that every section of an integrable function is integrable.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-multidimensional-integral-properties",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $Q=\\prod_{j<m}[a_j,b_j]$ be nondegenerate. For integrable\n$f,g:Q\\to\\mathbb R$ and scalars $\\alpha,\\beta$, the function\n$\\alpha f+\\beta g$ is integrable and its integral is\n$\\alpha\\int_Qf+\\beta\\int_Qg$. If $f\\le g$, then\n$\\int_Qf\\le\\int_Qg$. Also $|f|$ is integrable and\n$|\\int_Qf|\\le\\int_Q|f|$. If $a_r<c<b_r$, cutting $Q$ at the coordinate\nhyperplane $x_r=c$ gives two nondegenerate subrectangles; integrability on\n$Q$ is equivalent to integrability on both restrictions, and their integral\nvalues add to the integral over $Q$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $c \\in A$ be a limit point of $A$\n([[def-limit-point-r]]), let $f, g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be differentiable at $c$\n([[def-derivative]]) and let $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then:\n\n1. $f + g$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(f+g)'(c) = f'(c) + g'(c)$;\n2. $\\alpha f$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(\\alpha f)'(c) = \\alpha f'(c)$;\n3. $fg$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(fg)'(c) = f'(c)g(c) + f(c)g'(c)$;\n4. if $g(c) \\ne 0$ then, writing $A_0 := \\{\\, x \\in A : g(x) \\ne 0 \\,\\}$, the\n   point $c$ lies in $A_0$ and is a limit point of $A_0$, the quotient\n   $(f/g)|_{A_0} : A_0 \\to \\mathbb{R}$, $x \\mapsto f(x)/g(x)$, is differentiable\n   at $c$ as a function on $A_0$, and\n   $$\\bigl((f/g)|_{A_0}\\bigr)'(c) \\;=\\; \\frac{f'(c)\\,g(c) - f(c)\\,g'(c)}{g(c)^{2}} .$$\n\nEach claim asserts two things: that the derivative on the left exists, and that\nit has the stated value. Both are proved.\n\n**Why claim 4 is stated on $A_0$.** The function $f/g$ is not defined where $g$\nvanishes, and $g$ may vanish at points of $A$ far from $c$; restricting to $A_0$\nis forced. That the restriction still has $c$ as a limit point, so that a\nderivative there means anything at all, is not free either, and it is the last\nclaim of [[lem-sign-preservation-near-a-limit]] applied to $g$. The hypothesis\nis $g(c) \\ne 0$, not \"$g$ vanishes nowhere\".\n\n**Everything is proved through\n[[thm-caratheodory-characterisation]].** No difference quotient is estimated and\nno limit theorem beyond continuity is used, so no choice principle is spent.\nThe four identities are four algebraic rearrangements of an increment, each\nfollowed by a reading of [[thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-ftc-second-part",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a < b$ be reals, let $G : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be differentiable at every\npoint of $[a,b]$ as a function on $[a,b]$ ([[def-derivative]]; at $a$ and $b$\nthis is the one-sided derivative), let $f := G'$, and suppose $f$ is integrable\non $[a,b]$ ([[def-darboux-integral]]). Then\n\n$$\\int_a^b f \\;=\\; G(b) - G(a) .$$\n\n**Both hypotheses are needed and neither is removable.** A function may be\ndifferentiable everywhere with $G'$ not integrable — then the left-hand side does\nnot exist (an everywhere differentiable function with unbounded derivative) — and an\nintegrable $f$ need not be the derivative of anything\n(the sign function); both witnesses are on the\ncompanion page.\n\n**No continuity of $f$ is assumed**, which is what makes this the working form:\nthe theorem evaluates $\\int_a^b f$ for every integrable derivative, not only for\ncontinuous integrands.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "def-qft-regular-polynomial-functional-algebra",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $d\\ge1$, $X=\\mathbb R^d$, and $n\\ge1$. Write\n$\\mathcal D(X^n)=C_c^\\infty(X^n;\\mathbb C)$ for the complex test-function\nspace of smooth compactly supported functions\n([[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]]), with its LF topology\n([[def-test-function-topology]]). The convergence criterion used below is\nthat convergent test sequences have one common compact support and converge\nuniformly with every derivative\n([[thm-sequential-convergence-in-test-function-space]]). Here $C^\\infty$\nmeans every coordinate derivative exists and is continuous\n([[def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables]]). All integrals are\ncompact Riemann integrals: each integrand is continuous with compact support,\nso restrict it to a compact rectangle and apply\n[[thm-continuous-on-a-rectangle-is-riemann-integrable]]. Let\n$\\mathcal D_{\\mathrm{sym}}(X^n)$ be the subspace of kernels invariant under\nevery permutation of their $n$ $X$-variables. Fields are real smooth\nconfigurations $\\phi\\in C^\\infty(X;\\mathbb R)$. For an integer $N\\ge0$\nand a finite family\n$f_0\\in\\mathbb C$, $f_n\\in\\mathcal D_{\\mathrm{sym}}(X^n)$ for\n$1\\le n\\le N$, define\n$$ F_f(\\phi)=f_0+\\sum_{n=1}^{N}\\int_{X^n}f_n(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)\\prod_{j=1}^{n}\\phi(x_j)\\,dx_1\\cdots dx_n. $$\nThe pairing is complex-bilinear, with no conjugation on the kernel or field.\nAn element of $\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}(X)$ is this finite symmetric\ncoefficient family, evaluated on fields by $F_f$. Pointwise sums, scalar\nmultiples, and products again have finite symmetric compactly supported\ncoefficient families: for products, expand the two finite sums and\nsymmetrize each tensor product $f_m\\otimes g_n$. Thus these elements form\nan algebra; the product-integral identity follows from Riemann Fubini on a\nrectangle containing the compact supports\n([[thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles]]).\n\nFor $k=0$, set $F_f^{(0)}(\\phi)=F_f(\\phi)$. For $1\\le k\\le N$,\nthe $k$-th functional derivative kernel assigned to this coefficient\nfamily is\n$$ F_f^{(k)}(\\phi)(x_1,\\ldots,x_k)=\\sum_{n=k}^{N}\\frac{n!}{(n-k)!}\\int_{X^{n-k}}f_n(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)\\prod_{j=k+1}^{n}\\phi(x_j)\\,dx_{k+1}\\cdots dx_n. $$\nFor $k>N$ it is zero. Each displayed kernel is smooth, symmetric and compactly supported in its $k$ remaining variables: compactness follows by projection of the finite union of coefficient-kernel supports, and derivatives pass through the finite-compact integrals by [[thm-differentiation-under-the-integral-sign-on-a-compact-rectangle]].\nIn the term $n=k$, the empty integral and empty product mean simply\n$f_k(x_1,\\ldots,x_k)$.\n\nThe scope is exactly smooth compact coefficient kernels. A local functional\nwhose higher derivative kernels are distributions on a thin diagonal is not\nincluded merely because its field expression is polynomial.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Fix $\\Phi\\in\\mathcal D(X^n)$ and regard $X^n$ as $\\mathbb R^D$, $D=dn$. The coordinate projections of $\\operatorname{supp}\\Phi$ lie in a compact $K\\subset X$. By [F3] choose $\\chi\\in\\mathcal D(X)$ equal to one near $K$. Choose $L>0$ so large that every coordinate of $\\operatorname{supp}\\chi$ lies strictly inside $(-L/2,L/2)$. Extend $\\Phi$ by zero and then $L$-periodically in each of the $D$ coordinates; because its support is interior to the period cube, the resulting function is smooth on the compact $D$-torus.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "For $k\\in\\mathbb Z^D$, put $e_k(x)=\\exp(2\\pi i k\\cdot x/L)$, $Q=[-L/2,L/2]^D$, and $\\widehat\\Phi(k)=L^{-D}\\int_Q\\Phi(x)\\overline{e_k(x)}\\,dx$. These are Riemann integrals by [F7]. Periodic integration by parts in one coordinate has no boundary term: the endpoint product agrees by periodicity, and [F8] converts the integral of a derivative to that endpoint difference. Writing $\\Delta=\\sum_{j=1}^D\\partial_j^2$, applying this identity to $(1-\\Delta)^q\\Phi$, and using the character derivative from [F6], gives $\\widehat{(1-\\Delta)^q\\Phi}(k) =(1+(2\\pi/L)^2|k|^2)^q\\widehat\\Phi(k)$. The left side is bounded by the volume of $Q$ times the supremum of the continuous function $(1-\\Delta)^q\\Phi$, so $$ |\\widehat\\Phi(k)|\\le C_{\\Phi,q}\\bigl(1+|k|^2\\bigr)^{-q}. $$ For integer $m\\ge1$, the number of lattice points in $m\\le|k|_\\infty<m+1$ is at most $(2m+3)^D-(2m-1)^D\\le C_D(m+1)^{D-1}$, by expanding the two powers; the shell $|k|_\\infty<1$ is finite. Thus, for every multi-index $\\alpha$, choosing $q$ with $2q>D+|\\alpha|$ makes $\\sum_k |k|^{|\\alpha|}|\\widehat\\Phi(k)|$ finite. The Fourier series and each derivative series therefore converge absolutely and uniformly on the torus. Integrating each uniformly convergent derivative series along a coordinate segment and using [F8] shows it is the corresponding derivative of the uniformly convergent Fourier sum; iterating over coordinates gives the same assertion for every multi-index.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "algebra",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The uniform sum has the same Fourier coefficients as $\\Phi$, since uniform convergence allows integration term by term and distinct integer characters have zero cross integral by [F6]–[F8]. Their difference is a continuous $D$-periodic function with every multi-index Fourier coefficient zero. For $D=1$, all its Fejer means are finite sums of those zero coefficients, so [F5] makes the function zero. For $D>1$, fix the first frequency $k_1$ and integrate in the first coordinate to obtain a continuous $(D-1)$-periodic coefficient function; continuity follows from [F4] and the compact integral bound [F7]. Its remaining Fourier coefficients are the corresponding coefficients of the original difference by [F7], so induction makes each such coefficient function zero. Applying the one-dimensional case to each fixed choice of the remaining coordinates shows the original difference is zero. Hence the Fourier series sums to $\\Phi$, with all derivatives uniformly.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "step 1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Let $S_M\\Phi$ be the finite Fourier sum over $|k|_\\infty\\le M$. On $\\mathbb R^D$, put $$ P_M(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)=\\left(\\prod_{j=1}^{n}\\chi(x_j)\\right)S_M\\Phi(x_1,\\ldots,x_n). $$ Each Fourier character factors over the $n$ blocks $x_j\\in\\mathbb R^d$; after multiplication by the product cutoff, each term is an elementary tensor of tests in $\\mathcal D(X)$. Also $(\\prod_j\\chi(x_j))\\Phi=\\Phi$, since every coordinate projection of its support lies in $K$. The product rule [F8] and the derivative convergence from step 2.1 show $P_M\\to\\Phi$ uniformly with every derivative, with all supports in the fixed compact $(\\operatorname{supp}\\chi)^n$. By [F1] this is convergence in the LF topology. Applying the finite symmetrization operator preserves convergence and turns the approximants into symmetric finite tensor sums whenever $\\Phi$ is symmetric.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F8",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Suppose $\\sum_{n=0}^{N}\\int f_n(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)\\prod_j\\phi(x_j)\\,dx=0$ for every real smooth $\\phi$, with symmetric test kernels $f_n$ as in [F9]. Replacing $\\phi$ by $t\\phi$ makes a scalar polynomial in $t$ vanish for every real $t$, so each homogeneous term $\\int f_n\\phi^{\\otimes n}$ is zero for every $\\phi$. For fixed real test functions $h_1,\\ldots,h_n$, substitute $\\phi=\\sum_j t_jh_j$. The coefficient of $t_1\\cdots t_n$ is $n!\\int f_n(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)\\prod_jh_j(x_j)\\,dx$, by symmetry, and must vanish. Complex product tests are finite linear combinations of real product tests by separating real and imaginary parts. step 3.1 makes their span dense, so choose approximants $\\psi_M\\to\\overline{f_n}$ with support in a fixed compact rectangle $C$. Then $$ \\left|\\int f_n(\\psi_M-\\overline{f_n})\\right| \\le \\operatorname{vol}(C)\\|f_n\\|_\\infty \\|\\psi_M-\\overline{f_n}\\|_\\infty\\longrightarrow0. $$ Thus $\\int|f_n|^2=0$. If the continuous function $f_n$ were nonzero at some point, continuity would bound its modulus below by a positive constant on a ball, making this integral positive; hence $f_n=0$. The constant term is zero by taking $\\phi=0$. This proves uniqueness and the well-definedness claim used by the definition.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F9",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.1 handles the zero test with empty support and zero approximants."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 4.1 isolates the constant coefficient by evaluating at the zero field."
        },
