    "thm-qft-regular-wick-star-associativity": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qft-regular-polynomial-functional-algebra",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $d\\ge1$, $X=\\mathbb R^d$, and $n\\ge1$. Write\n$\\mathcal D(X^n)=C_c^\\infty(X^n;\\mathbb C)$ for the complex test-function\nspace of smooth compactly supported functions\n([[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]]), with its LF topology\n([[def-test-function-topology]]). The convergence criterion used below is\nthat convergent test sequences have one common compact support and converge\nuniformly with every derivative\n([[thm-sequential-convergence-in-test-function-space]]). Here $C^\\infty$\nmeans every coordinate derivative exists and is continuous\n([[def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables]]). All integrals are\ncompact Riemann integrals: each integrand is continuous with compact support,\nso restrict it to a compact rectangle and apply\n[[thm-continuous-on-a-rectangle-is-riemann-integrable]]. Let\n$\\mathcal D_{\\mathrm{sym}}(X^n)$ be the subspace of kernels invariant under\nevery permutation of their $n$ $X$-variables. Fields are real smooth\nconfigurations $\\phi\\in C^\\infty(X;\\mathbb R)$. For an integer $N\\ge0$\nand a finite family\n$f_0\\in\\mathbb C$, $f_n\\in\\mathcal D_{\\mathrm{sym}}(X^n)$ for\n$1\\le n\\le N$, define\n$$ F_f(\\phi)=f_0+\\sum_{n=1}^{N}\\int_{X^n}f_n(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)\\prod_{j=1}^{n}\\phi(x_j)\\,dx_1\\cdots dx_n. $$\nThe pairing is complex-bilinear, with no conjugation on the kernel or field.\nAn element of $\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}(X)$ is this finite symmetric\ncoefficient family, evaluated on fields by $F_f$. Pointwise sums, scalar\nmultiples, and products again have finite symmetric compactly supported\ncoefficient families: for products, expand the two finite sums and\nsymmetrize each tensor product $f_m\\otimes g_n$. Thus these elements form\nan algebra; the product-integral identity follows from Riemann Fubini on a\nrectangle containing the compact supports\n([[thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles]]).\n\nFor $k=0$, set $F_f^{(0)}(\\phi)=F_f(\\phi)$. For $1\\le k\\le N$,\nthe $k$-th functional derivative kernel assigned to this coefficient\nfamily is\n$$ F_f^{(k)}(\\phi)(x_1,\\ldots,x_k)=\\sum_{n=k}^{N}\\frac{n!}{(n-k)!}\\int_{X^{n-k}}f_n(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)\\prod_{j=k+1}^{n}\\phi(x_j)\\,dx_{k+1}\\cdots dx_n. $$\nFor $k>N$ it is zero. Each displayed kernel is smooth, symmetric and compactly supported in its $k$ remaining variables: compactness follows by projection of the finite union of coefficient-kernel supports, and derivatives pass through the finite-compact integrals by [[thm-differentiation-under-the-integral-sign-on-a-compact-rectangle]].\nIn the term $n=k$, the empty integral and empty product mean simply\n$f_k(x_1,\\ldots,x_k)$.\n\nThe scope is exactly smooth compact coefficient kernels. A local functional\nwhose higher derivative kernels are distributions on a thin diagonal is not\nincluded merely because its field expression is polynomial.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-qft-tensor-test-function-density",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $d,n\\ge1$, $X=\\mathbb R^d$, and give\n$\\mathcal D(X^n)=C_c^\\infty(X^n;\\mathbb C)$ its LF test-function topology.\nThe finite linear span of elementary product tests\n$$ (x_1,\\ldots,x_n)\\longmapsto\\prod_{j=1}^{n}g_j(x_j),\\qquad g_j\\in\\mathcal D(X), $$\nis dense. More precisely, for each $\\Phi\\in\\mathcal D(X^n)$, there is a\nsequence of such finite sums whose supports lie in one fixed compact product\nand whose derivatives of every multi-index converge uniformly to those of\n$\\Phi$. Symmetrized finite sums are dense in\n$\\mathcal D_{\\mathrm{sym}}(X^n)$. Consequently the symmetric-kernel\nrepresentation in [[def-qft-regular-polynomial-functional-algebra]] is\nunique: if a finite symmetric-kernel polynomial vanishes on every real smooth\nfield, every coefficient kernel vanishes.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-tensor-product-distributions-and-iterated-pairings",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $u\\in\\mathcal D'(U)$ and $v\\in\\mathcal D'(V)$ the tensor candidate is a distribution on $U\\times V$, uniquely determined by $(u\\otimes v)(\\varphi\\otimes\\psi)=u(\\varphi)v(\\psi)$. Its two iterated pairing orders agree, and $\\operatorname{supp}(u\\otimes v)=\\operatorname{supp}u\\times\\operatorname{supp}v$. Three-factor pairings associate. Moreover $\\partial_x^\\alpha(u\\otimes v)=(\\partial^\\alpha u)\\otimes v$ and $\\partial_y^\\beta(u\\otimes v)=u\\otimes(\\partial^\\beta v)$. All claims hold in ZF.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-distribution-pairing-with-smooth-parameter-families",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $r,n\\ge1$ be integers, let $X\\subseteq\\mathbb R^r$ and $Y\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, let $u\\in\\mathcal D'(Y)$, and let $F\\in C^\\infty(X\\times Y;\\mathbb C)$. Suppose that for each compact $H\\subseteq X$ there is compact $K\\subseteq Y$ with $\\operatorname{supp}_y F(x,\\cdot)\\subseteq K$ for every $x\\in H$. Then $a(x)=u(F(x,\\cdot))$ is smooth and $\\partial_x^\\alpha a(x)=u(\\partial_x^\\alpha F(x,\\cdot))$.\n\nAssume Countable Choice for the following integral clause. For every compact measurable $E\\subseteq X$, the function $G(y)=\\int_E F(x,y)\\,dx$ is a test in $\\mathcal D(Y)$, its $y$ derivatives pass under the integral, and $u(G)=\\int_E u(F(x,\\cdot))\\,dx$. Integrals here are Lebesgue integrals. The smoothness and differentiation claims hold in ZF; Countable Choice supplies Lebesgue measure for the integral clause. Neither clause uses tensor products or distributional mollification.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For each $k$, [F2] constructs $W^{\\otimes k}$ on the independent variable pairs. The kernels $F^{(k)}(\\phi)$ and $G^{(k)}(\\phi)$ in [F1] are smooth and compactly supported, so the displayed pairing is defined. In each homogeneous term of $F$ and $G$, pairing the $k$ contracted variables leaves a smooth compact kernel in the remaining variables: the support lies in the projection of the original compact coefficient supports, and [F3] passes every remaining-variable derivative through the pairing. Thus each summand is again a regular polynomial functional.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "If $F$ and $G$ have degrees $m$ and $n$, then $F^{(k)}=0$ for $k>m$ and $G^{(k)}=0$ for $k>n$. Hence only $0\\le k\\le\\min(m,n)$ contribute; the sum is finite, independent of a coefficient presentation by [F1], and belongs to $\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}(X)[\\eta]$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "For $f\\in\\mathcal D(X)$, use the auxiliary formal generator $E_f(s,\\phi)=\\exp(s\\ell_f(\\phi))$; it is a generating series and is not asserted to be an element of $\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}(X)$. Its $k$-th derivative in $\\phi$ is $s^k f^{\\otimes k}E_f$. Substitution into the finite coefficient formula gives, coefficientwise in $s,t,\\eta$, $$ E_f(s)\\star_W E_g(t)=\\exp\\!\\bigl(\\eta st W(f,g)\\bigr)E_{sf+tg}. $$",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Applying step 2.2 twice, both bracketings of three generators equal $$ \\exp\\!\\bigl(\\eta(stW(f,g)+srW(f,h)+trW(g,h))\\bigr)E_{sf+tg+rh}, $$ by bilinearity of $W$. Comparing coefficients of $s,t,r$ proves associativity for powers of linear functionals. For distinct tests in one input, replace $f$ by $\\sum_{j=1}^m s_jf_j$ in the same coefficientwise identity; its coefficient of $s_1\\cdots s_m$ is the symmetrized product $\\ell_{f_1}\\cdots\\ell_{f_m}$. Make this substitution independently in each of the three inputs. Thus the associator vanishes for every symmetrized elementary tensor-product coefficient kernel and for their finite sums.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "By [F1], symmetrized finite product-test kernels are dense in every coefficient test space. Each fixed coefficient of the associator is a finite sum of compositions of continuous tensor-distribution pairings from [F2] and smooth parameter pairings from [F3], hence is continuous in the coefficient kernels. Passing to the limits of the product-test approximants from the preceding coefficient comparison proves associativity for arbitrary $F,G,H\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}(X)$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "For linear functionals only $k=0,1$ occur. The $k=0$ term cancels in the commutator, while the $k=1$ terms are $\\eta W(f,g)$ and $\\eta W(g,f)$; this gives the displayed commutator. If $W$ is Hermitian, conjugating each finite contraction reverses its two variable groups and gives $(F\\star_W G)^*=G^*\\star_W F^*$.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 2.1 includes degree-zero inputs and the k=0 ordinary product term."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Steps 1.1 and 2.1 cover W=0 and the zero polynomial in the finite contraction formula."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 5.1 checks the commutator for linear generators."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Steps 1.1 and 2.1 cover degrees below the contraction order through vanishing higher derivatives."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No interval or endpoint is part of this algebraic statement."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Statement of Regular Wick-star contractions are well defined and associative: Let $X=\\mathbb R^d$, $d\\ge1$, and let $W\\in\\mathcal D'(X\\times X)$ be any continuous complex bidistribution. For a real formal parameter $\\eta$ and $F,G\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}(X)$, define $$ F\\star_W G=\\sum_{k\\ge0}\\frac{\\eta^k}{k!}\\left\\langle W^{\\otimes k},F^{(k)}(\\phi)\\otimes G^{(k)}(\\phi)\\right\\rangle . $$ The pairing is over $k$ independent pairs of variables; $W^{\\otimes k}$ is formed by iterated distributional tensor products, not by multiplying distributions at coincident points. The sum is finite because the inputs are polynomial. It defines an element of $\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}(X)[\\eta]$, and $\\star_W$ is associative. If $W$ is Hermitian in the sense $\\overline{\\langle W,\\overline g\\otimes\\over The displayed finite algebraic/test-contraction computation uses supplied objects rather than choosing elements of a family of nonempty sets; no nonempty-family selection is a separate conclusion."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Statement of Regular Wick-star contractions are well defined and associative: Let $X=\\mathbb R^d$, $d\\ge1$, and let $W\\in\\mathcal D'(X\\times X)$ be any continuous complex bidistribution. For a real formal parameter $\\eta$ and $F,G\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}(X)$, define $$ F\\star_W G=\\sum_{k\\ge0}\\frac{\\eta^k}{k!}\\left\\langle W^{\\otimes k},F^{(k)}(\\phi)\\otimes G^{(k)}(\\phi)\\right\\rangle . $$ The pairing is over $k$ independent pairs of variables; $W^{\\otimes k}$ is formed by iterated distributional tensor products, not by multiplying distributions at coincident points. The sum is finite because the inputs are polynomial. It defines an element of $\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}(X)[\\eta]$, and $\\star_W$ is associative. If $W$ is Hermitian in the sense $\\overline{\\langle W,\\overline g\\otimes\\over The forward iff axis adds no separately stated converse hypothesis here; the recorded claim is the displayed construction, identity, implication or scope qualification, not a characterization by a new necessary-and-sufficient condition."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Statement of Regular Wick-star contractions are well defined and associative: Let $X=\\mathbb R^d$, $d\\ge1$, and let $W\\in\\mathcal D'(X\\times X)$ be any continuous complex bidistribution. For a real formal parameter $\\eta$ and $F,G\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}(X)$, define $$ F\\star_W G=\\sum_{k\\ge0}\\frac{\\eta^k}{k!}\\left\\langle W^{\\otimes k},F^{(k)}(\\phi)\\otimes G^{(k)}(\\phi)\\right\\rangle . $$ The pairing is over $k$ independent pairs of variables; $W^{\\otimes k}$ is formed by iterated distributional tensor products, not by multiplying distributions at coincident points. The sum is finite because the inputs are polynomial. It defines an element of $\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}(X)[\\eta]$, and $\\star_W$ is associative. If $W$ is Hermitian in the sense $\\overline{\\langle W,\\overline g\\otimes\\over The reverse iff axis adds no separately stated converse hypothesis here; the recorded claim is the displayed construction, identity, implication or scope qualification, not a characterization by a new necessary-and-sufficient condition."
        }
