        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-distribution-pairing-with-smooth-parameter-families",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $r,n\\ge1$ be integers, let $X\\subseteq\\mathbb R^r$ and $Y\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, let $u\\in\\mathcal D'(Y)$, and let $F\\in C^\\infty(X\\times Y;\\mathbb C)$. Suppose that for each compact $H\\subseteq X$ there is compact $K\\subseteq Y$ with $\\operatorname{supp}_y F(x,\\cdot)\\subseteq K$ for every $x\\in H$. Then $a(x)=u(F(x,\\cdot))$ is smooth and $\\partial_x^\\alpha a(x)=u(\\partial_x^\\alpha F(x,\\cdot))$.\n\nAssume Countable Choice for the following integral clause. For every compact measurable $E\\subseteq X$, the function $G(y)=\\int_E F(x,y)\\,dx$ is a test in $\\mathcal D(Y)$, its $y$ derivatives pass under the integral, and $u(G)=\\int_E u(F(x,\\cdot))\\,dx$. Integrals here are Lebesgue integrals. The smoothness and differentiation claims hold in ZF; Countable Choice supplies Lebesgue measure for the integral clause. Neither clause uses tensor products or distributional mollification.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-qft-regular-wick-star-associativity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X=\\mathbb R^d$, $d\\ge1$, and let $W\\in\\mathcal D'(X\\times X)$ be\nany continuous complex bidistribution. For a real formal parameter $\\eta$\nand $F,G\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}(X)$, define\n$$ F\\star_W G=\\sum_{k\\ge0}\\frac{\\eta^k}{k!}\\left\\langle W^{\\otimes k},F^{(k)}(\\phi)\\otimes G^{(k)}(\\phi)\\right\\rangle . $$\nThe pairing is over $k$ independent pairs of variables; $W^{\\otimes k}$\nis formed by iterated distributional tensor products, not by multiplying\ndistributions at coincident points. The sum is finite because the inputs are\npolynomial. It defines an element of\n$\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}(X)[\\eta]$, and $\\star_W$ is associative.\nIf $W$ is Hermitian in the sense\n$\\overline{\\langle W,\\overline g\\otimes\\overline f\\rangle}\n=\\langle W,f\\otimes g\\rangle$, then\n$(F\\star_W G)^*=G^*\\star_W F^*$, where $F^*(\\phi)=\\overline{F(\\phi)}$.\nFor $\\ell_f(\\phi)=\\int_X f(x)\\phi(x)\\,dx$,\n$$ [\\ell_f,\\ell_g]_{\\star_W}=\\eta\\bigl(W(f,g)-W(g,f)\\bigr), \\qquad W(f,g):=\\langle W,f\\otimes g\\rangle . $$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For a homogeneous coefficient of degree $m$, one application of $\\Gamma_K$ contracts two derivative variables against $K$, leaving a smooth compact kernel by [F1]–[F3]. It lowers the polynomial degree by two; therefore $\\Gamma_K^jF=0$ when $2j>\\deg F$, so $\\alpha_KF$ is a finite sum in $\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}(X)[\\eta]$. The same finite argument defines $\\alpha_{-K}$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "On the auxiliary formal generator $E_f(s,\\phi)=\\exp(s\\ell_f(\\phi))$, each second derivative contracts to $K(f,f)E_f$. Coefficientwise this gives $$ \\alpha_KE_f(s)=\\exp\\!\\left(\\frac{\\eta s^2}{2}K(f,f)\\right)E_f(s). $$ The exponential is formal; each coefficient used below involves only finitely many derivatives.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "On two auxiliary generators, the left side of the asserted identity has the scalar factor $$ \\exp\\!\\left(\\eta stW(f,g)+\\frac{\\eta}{2}K(sf+tg,sf+tg)\\right). $$ The right side has factor $$ \\exp\\!\\left(\\frac{\\eta s^2}{2}K(f,f)+\\frac{\\eta t^2}{2}K(g,g)+\\eta st(W+K)(f,g)\\right). $$ Symmetry and bilinearity of $K$ make these exponents equal.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Comparing coefficients of $s,t$ in step 3.1 first proves the identity for powers of linear functionals. Replacing $f$ by $\\sum_j s_jf_j$ and comparing the coefficient of $s_1\\cdots s_m$, and doing the same independently for $g$, proves it for every finite product of distinct linear functionals. Each fixed coefficient is continuous in its smooth compact kernels by [F2]–[F4]; the density in [F1] therefore extends the identity to all $F,G\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}(X)$. For an input of degree $d$, the coefficient of $\\Gamma_K^r$ in $\\alpha_K\\alpha_{-K}$ is multiplied by $\\sum_{j=0}^r\\binom rj(-1)^{r-j}=(1-1)^r$, which is zero for $r>0$ and one for $r=0$. The sums are finite because $\\Gamma_K$ lowers degree by two, so $\\alpha_K\\alpha_{-K}=I$; the reverse composition is identical.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.1 handles degree-zero polynomials, on which both contraction exponentials are the identity."
        },
