        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Statement of Regular normal-ordering change is an exact star isomorphism: Let $W\\in\\mathcal D'(X\\times X)$, $X=\\mathbb R^d$, and let $K\\in\\mathcal D'(X\\times X)$ be symmetric under interchange of its variables. Define $$ \\Gamma_KF(\\phi)=\\langle K,F^{(2)}(\\phi)\\rangle,\\qquad \\alpha_K=\\exp\\!\\left(\\frac{\\eta}{2}\\Gamma_K\\right) =\\sum_{j\\ge0}\\frac{(\\eta/2)^j}{j!}\\Gamma_K^j $$ on $\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}(X)[\\eta]$. Each sum is finite on each polynomial because $\\Gamma_K$ lowers degree by two. Then $\\alpha_K$ is invertible with inverse $\\alpha_{-K}$, and $$ \\alpha_K(F\\star_W G)=\\alpha_K(F)\\star_{W+K}\\alpha_K(G) $$ for all regular polynomial functionals $F,G$. Thus the change from $W$ to $W+K$ is an exact algebra isomorphism on this regular class. The displayed finite algebraic/test-contraction computation uses supplied objects rather than choosing elements of a family of nonempty sets; no nonempty-family selection is a separate conclusion."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The lemma states no biconditional."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The lemma states no biconditional."
        }
      ],
      "finite_checks": [
        {
          "check": "Apply alpha_K alpha_-K to ell_f ell_g, whose second derivative is f⊗g+g⊗f.",
          "result": "Symmetry gives Gamma_K(ell_f ell_g)=2K(f,g), so the two degree-zero shifts +eta K(f,g) and -eta K(f,g) cancel.",
          "verifies": "The finite inverse-composition argument in proof step 4.1."
        }
      ]
    },
    "thm-qft-regular-formal-causal-factorization": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-qft-free-time-ordered-kernel",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Fix the scalar shell parameter $\\mu\\ge0$ and all shell, Fourier, and metric conventions of [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]], with $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ and $\\Box=-\\partial_t^2+\\Delta_{\\mathbf x}$. For $\\phi\\in\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^4)$ define the partial spatial Fourier transform\n$$\\widehat\\phi_t(\\mathbf k)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}e^{-i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}\\phi(t,\\mathbf x)\\,d^3\\mathbf x$$\nand define $H_\\mu\\in\\mathcal S'(\\mathbb R^4)$ by\n$$\\langle H_\\mu,\\phi\\rangle=\\int_{\\mathbb R}dt\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3\\,2\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)}e^{-i\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)|t|}\\widehat\\phi_t(-\\mathbf k).$$\nThis is a continuous distributional extension of the free scalar time-ordered two-point function: on $t>0$ its kernel is the Wightman two-point distribution $W_\\mu(t,\\mathbf x)$, and on $t<0$ it is $W_\\mu(-t,-\\mathbf x)$. The displayed partial Fourier formula fixes the extension at $t=0$; no product of a step function with an unspecified singular distribution is used. The kernel is symmetric under $(t,\\mathbf x)\\mapsto(-t,-\\mathbf x)$, agrees with the two orderings on spacelike-separated supports, and obeys\n$$ (\\Box-\\mu^2)H_\\mu=i\\delta_0 $$\nin $\\mathcal S'(\\mathbb R^4)$. There is no extra prefactor $i$ in the definition of $H_\\mu$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], let $\\mu\\ge0$, let\n$H_\\mu$ and $K_\\mu$ be as in [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]], and form\n$\\mathcal F_s(H_\\mu)$ with vacuum $\\Omega$ and finite-particle domain\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$. For $f\\in\\mathscr S(\\mathbb R^4)$ define on\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$\n$$\\Phi_\\mu(f)=a(K_\\mu\\overline f)+a^\\dagger(K_\\mu f).$$\nThis is a complex-linear map of test functions into operators\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}\\to\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$. For real $f$ it is symmetric\nand equals $\\sqrt2\\,\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(K_\\mu f)|_{\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}}$, where the Segal field of\n[[lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain]] is\n$\\Phi_{\\rm Segal}(h)=\\overline{(a^\\dagger(h)+a(h))/\\sqrt2}$.\nThus its Weyl unitary is\n$W_\\mu(f):=W_{\\rm Segal}(\\sqrt2 K_\\mu f)$ and, for real $f,g$,\n$$W_\\mu(f)W_\\mu(g)= e^{-i\\,\\operatorname{Im}\\langle K_\\mu f,K_\\mu g\\rangle_2}W_\\mu(f+g).$$\nFor every real $f$ the vacuum Weyl expectation is\n$$\\langle\\Omega,W_\\mu(f)\\Omega\\rangle_2 =e^{-\\|K_\\mu f\\|_2^2/2}.$$\nHere the inner product is second-variable-linear as fixed in\n[[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. The vacuum has zero one-point function and\ntwo-point function\n$$\\langle\\Omega,\\Phi_\\mu(f)\\Phi_\\mu(g)\\Omega\\rangle_2 =\\langle K_\\mu\\overline f,K_\\mu g\\rangle_2,$$\nwhich obeys $\\langle\\Omega,\\Phi_\\mu(\\overline f)\\Phi_\\mu(f)\\Omega\\rangle_2=\\|K_\\mu f\\|_2^2\\ge0$ for every complex test $f$ (ordinary diagonal positivity holds for real tests); higher vacuum moments of ordered real smeared\nfields obey the CCR pairing recursion and hence the free-field Wick pairing\nrule. The field is a weak operator-valued tempered distribution on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$: every matrix coefficient $f\\mapsto\\langle\\Psi,\\Phi_\\mu(f)\\Theta\\rangle_2$ for $\\Psi,\\Theta\\in\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ is a continuous complex-linear functional on Schwartz space. Its adjoint satisfies $\\Phi_\\mu(f)^*|_{\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}}=\\Phi_\\mu(\\overline f)$, and its normalized vacuum defines a positive state on the finite polynomial $*$-algebra generated by these fields.\n\nThe relative-coordinate two-point tempered distribution is\n$$W_\\mu(z)=\\int_{H_\\mu^+}e^{-ip\\circ z}\\,d\\nu_\\mu(p),$$\nunderstood by pairing with Schwartz tests. Its translation-invariant bidistribution is the shell integral of $e^{-ip\\circ x}e^{ip\\circ y}$; on product tests it is the displayed vacuum covariance.\n\nFor $g=(a,\\Lambda)$, let $f_g$ and $U_\\mu(g)$ be the affine test pullback\nand one-particle action of [[lem-qft-scalar-poincare-action]]. Their Fock\nlifts implement the same covariance for $\\Phi_\\mu$ on $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$\nand for the bounded Weyl operators.\n\nFor translations, the joint spectral measure on Fock space is the\nsecond-quantized multiplier measure with one-particle vector\n$(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k),-\\mathbf k)$. Its support is contained in the closed\nfuture cone $\\{(E,\\mathbf P):E\\ge|\\mathbf P|\\}$; the vacuum is an eigenvector\nat $(0,\\mathbf0)$. These are conditional mathematical model deductions.\nNo point field, empirical observation or measurement claim is made.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-qft-scalar-causal-commutator",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. For $\\mu\\ge0$, define the tempered distribution\n$$\\Delta_\\mu(t,\\mathbf x)= \\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3} \\frac{\\sin(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)t)}{\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)} e^{i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x},$$\nwith the massless integrand defined off $\\mathbf k=0$; its $1/|\\mathbf k|$\nbehavior is locally integrable in three dimensions. Then\n$$\\operatorname{supp}\\Delta_\\mu \\subseteq\\{(t,\\mathbf x):t^2-|\\mathbf x|^2\\ge0\\},$$\nthe closed causal cone for the $(-+++)$ metric. For real compactly supported\nsmooth $f,g$, put\n$$\\Delta_\\mu(f,g)= \\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\!\\int_{\\mathbb R^4} f(x)g(y)\\Delta_\\mu(x-y)\\,d^4x\\,d^4y.$$\nOn $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ the smeared fields of\n[[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]] satisfy\n$$[\\Phi_\\mu(f),\\Phi_\\mu(g)]=-i\\Delta_\\mu(f,g)I.$$\nConsequently spacelike-separated supports give commuting fields.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-qft-regular-formal-time-ordering",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix $\\mu\\ge0$ and the $(-+++)$, shell, Fourier, and coordinate conventions\nof [[lem-qft-free-time-ordered-kernel]]. That current supplier states that\nits relative-coordinate tempered distribution $H_\\mu$ is symmetric, agrees\nwith the ordered Wightman kernels on strictly ordered time supports and on\nspacelike-separated supports, and has no prefactor $i$ in its definition.\nIts current carrier is a draft with a supplied conditional proof; the formula\nbelow uses that exact interface.\n\nFor open $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$, $\\mathcal D(\\Omega)$ is the space\nof smooth compactly supported complex tests with the LF topology, and\n$\\mathcal D'(\\Omega)$ is its continuous complex-linear dual, paired\nbilinearly with no conjugation\n([[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]], [[def-test-function-topology]],\n[[def-distribution]]). Schwartz space consists of smooth functions with all\npolynomially weighted derivatives bounded; $\\mathcal S'$ is its continuous\ncomplex-linear dual with bilinear pairing\n([[def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms]],\n[[def-schwartz-topology-and-convergence]], [[def-tempered-distribution]]).\n\nFor any relative-coordinate distribution $K\\in\\mathcal S'(\\mathbb R^4)$,\ndefine its translation-invariant two-point bidistribution\n$\\widetilde K\\in\\mathcal D'(\\mathbb R^4\\times\\mathbb R^4)$ by\n$$ \\langle\\widetilde K,\\Psi\\rangle =\\left\\langle K,\\ z\\longmapsto\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\Psi(y+z,y)\\,dy\\right\\rangle, \\qquad \\Psi\\in\\mathcal D(\\mathbb R^4\\times\\mathbb R^4). $$\nThe inner function is a test function: its support lies in the difference\nof the two compact coordinate projections of $\\operatorname{supp}\\Psi$;\neach derivative in $z$ is the Riemann integral of the corresponding\nfirst-variable derivative of $\\Psi$ over a compact rectangle containing\nthe fixed compact second-variable projection\n([[thm-continuous-on-a-rectangle-is-riemann-integrable]]).\nOn each fixed compact support space, every output derivative seminorm is\nbounded by the volume of a compact rectangle containing the second-variable\nprojection times the corresponding source derivative seminorm. The support\nof the output lies in the compact difference of the two projections. Thus\nthis map is continuous on each fixed-support test space, and hence on the LF\ntest topology [[def-test-function-topology]].\nThus the displayed formula defines a distribution without multiplying\nsingular distributions. Write $\\mathsf H_\\mu=\\widetilde H_\\mu$.\n\nFor $F\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}(\\mathbb R^4)$, define its support as the\nfinite union of the coordinate projections of the supports of its nonconstant\nsymmetric coefficient kernels. This is a compact set and is presentation\nindependent by [[lem-qft-tensor-test-function-density]]. For compact\n$A,B\\subset\\mathbb R^4$, write $A\\succcurlyeq_{\\mathrm{out}} B$ when\n$$ x-y\\notin C_-:=\\{(t,\\mathbf x)\\in\\mathbb R^4:t\\le-|\\mathbf x|\\} \\quad\\text{for every }x\\in A,\\ y\\in B. $$\nThis means the first support has no point in the closed causal past of the\nsecond, using the supplier's fixed inertial chart.\n\nFor $n\\ge2$ and $1\\le i<j\\le n$, let $D_{\\mathsf H}^{ij}$ contract one\nfunctional derivative in slots $i,j$ against $\\mathsf H_\\mu$, pairing\nindependent variables. Define\n$$ T_n^H(F_1,\\ldots,F_n)(\\phi) =\\left.m_n\\exp\\!\\left(\\eta\\sum_{1\\le i<j\\le n}D_{\\mathsf H}^{ij}\\right) \\prod_{i=1}^nF_i(\\phi_i)\\right|_{\\phi_1=\\cdots=\\phi_n=\\phi}, \\qquad T_0^H=1,\\quad T_1^H(F)=F. $$\nHere $m_n$ multiplies the remaining factors and then substitutes the same\nfield $\\phi$ in every slot; on coefficient kernels it symmetrizes their\nfinite tensor products. The parameter $\\eta$ is formal and fixed by\ncomplex conjugation. Each contraction removes two polynomial degrees, so the\nexponential is a finite sum on polynomial inputs. The dependencies\n[[thm-tensor-product-distributions-and-iterated-pairings]] and\n[[lem-distribution-pairing-with-smooth-parameter-families]] make every\ncontraction well defined and leave smooth compact kernels in the uncontracted\nvariables; no diagonal product occurs. Symmetry of $\\mathsf H_\\mu$ makes\n$T_n^H$ symmetric in its inputs.\n\nFor a regular compactly supported interaction $V$ and formal coupling $z$,\ndefine\n$$ S_z^H(V)=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{(iz/\\eta)^n}{n!}\\,T_n^H(V,\\ldots,V) \\quad\\text{with coefficients in }\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}(\\mathbb R^4)[\\eta,\\eta^{-1}]. $$\nThis is a coefficientwise formal series; the definition asserts no\nconvergence in $z$, no time-ordered product for singular local functionals,\nand no continuum interacting field.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-qft-regular-wick-star-associativity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X=\\mathbb R^d$, $d\\ge1$, and let $W\\in\\mathcal D'(X\\times X)$ be\nany continuous complex bidistribution. For a real formal parameter $\\eta$\nand $F,G\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}(X)$, define\n$$ F\\star_W G=\\sum_{k\\ge0}\\frac{\\eta^k}{k!}\\left\\langle W^{\\otimes k},F^{(k)}(\\phi)\\otimes G^{(k)}(\\phi)\\right\\rangle . $$\nThe pairing is over $k$ independent pairs of variables; $W^{\\otimes k}$\nis formed by iterated distributional tensor products, not by multiplying\ndistributions at coincident points. The sum is finite because the inputs are\npolynomial. It defines an element of\n$\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}(X)[\\eta]$, and $\\star_W$ is associative.\nIf $W$ is Hermitian in the sense\n$\\overline{\\langle W,\\overline g\\otimes\\overline f\\rangle}\n=\\langle W,f\\otimes g\\rangle$, then\n$(F\\star_W G)^*=G^*\\star_W F^*$, where $F^*(\\phi)=\\overline{F(\\phi)}$.\nFor $\\ell_f(\\phi)=\\int_X f(x)\\phi(x)\\,dx$,\n$$ [\\ell_f,\\ell_g]_{\\star_W}=\\eta\\bigl(W(f,g)-W(g,f)\\bigr), \\qquad W(f,g):=\\langle W,f\\otimes g\\rangle . $$",
          "uses": [
            "4.1",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-qft-tensor-test-function-density",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $d,n\\ge1$, $X=\\mathbb R^d$, and give\n$\\mathcal D(X^n)=C_c^\\infty(X^n;\\mathbb C)$ its LF test-function topology.\nThe finite linear span of elementary product tests\n$$ (x_1,\\ldots,x_n)\\longmapsto\\prod_{j=1}^{n}g_j(x_j),\\qquad g_j\\in\\mathcal D(X), $$\nis dense. More precisely, for each $\\Phi\\in\\mathcal D(X^n)$, there is a\nsequence of such finite sums whose supports lie in one fixed compact product\nand whose derivatives of every multi-index converge uniformly to those of\n$\\Phi$. Symmetrized finite sums are dense in\n$\\mathcal D_{\\mathrm{sym}}(X^n)$. Consequently the symmetric-kernel\nrepresentation in [[def-qft-regular-polynomial-functional-algebra]] is\nunique: if a finite symmetric-kernel polynomial vanishes on every real smooth\nfield, every coefficient kernel vanishes.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "lem-test-function-cutoffs-and-euclidean-localization",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In ZF, for compact $K\\subseteq\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ with $\\Omega$ open, there is $\\chi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\Omega)$ with $0\\le\\chi\\le1$ and $\\chi=1$ on a neighborhood of $K$. Every open cover of $\\Omega$ admits an at most countable locally finite smooth partition of unity with compact supports, each support contained in some cover member. Subordination here asserts existence of such a member for each support; it does not select cover labels.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-tensor-product-distributions-and-iterated-pairings",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $u\\in\\mathcal D'(U)$ and $v\\in\\mathcal D'(V)$ the tensor candidate is a distribution on $U\\times V$, uniquely determined by $(u\\otimes v)(\\varphi\\otimes\\psi)=u(\\varphi)v(\\psi)$. Its two iterated pairing orders agree, and $\\operatorname{supp}(u\\otimes v)=\\operatorname{supp}u\\times\\operatorname{supp}v$. Three-factor pairings associate. Moreover $\\partial_x^\\alpha(u\\otimes v)=(\\partial^\\alpha u)\\otimes v$ and $\\partial_y^\\beta(u\\otimes v)=u\\otimes(\\partial^\\beta v)$. All claims hold in ZF.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Taking vacuum expectations in the commutator of [F3] and using the covariance in [F2] gives $W_\\mu(f,g)-W_\\mu(g,f)=-i\\Delta_\\mu(f,g)$ for real compact tests. Complex bilinearity extends this equality to complex product tests, and density [F6] extends it to the lifted bidistributions: $\\widetilde W_\\mu-\\widetilde W_\\mu^{\\mathsf T}=-i\\widetilde\\Delta_\\mu$. If $\\Psi$ is supported where $x-y$ is spacelike, the relative test $z\\mapsto\\int\\Psi(y+z,y)\\,dy$ is compactly supported in the spacelike open set. By [F3] its pairing with $\\Delta_\\mu$ is zero. Thus $\\widetilde W_\\mu-\\widetilde W_\\mu^{\\mathsf T}$ vanishes there. The conclusion is conditional on the current QFT-MF02 statements.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F6",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Put $C=A-B$. Every $z=x-y\\in C$ either has $z^0>0$ or is spacelike: if $z^0\\le0$ and it is not spacelike, then $z\\in C_-$, excluded by $A\\succcurlyeq_{\\mathrm{out}}B$; this also excludes the zero difference. Thus $C$ lies in the open set $\\Omega=U_+\\cup U_{\\mathrm{sp}}$, where $U_+=\\{z:z^0>0\\}$ and $U_{\\mathrm{sp}}=\\{z:(z^0)^2-|\\mathbf z|^2<0\\}$. By [F7], the cover $\\{U_+,U_{\\mathrm{sp}}\\}$ of $\\Omega$ has a locally finite smooth partition of unity subordinate to these sets. Any compactly supported relative test in $\\Omega$ meets only finitely many partition functions. On each $U_+$ piece [F1] gives $H_\\mu=W_\\mu$; on each $U_{\\mathrm{sp}}$ piece [F1] gives agreement with both Wightman orderings, which coincide there by step 1.1. Therefore $\\widetilde H_\\mu-\\widetilde W_\\mu$ vanishes on $\\Omega$. The open set $O=\\{(x,y):x-y\\in\\Omega\\}$ contains $A\\times B$, and the lift [F4] sends every test supported in $O$ to a relative test supported in $\\Omega$. Hence $\\widetilde H_\\mu-\\widetilde W_\\mu$ vanishes on an open neighborhood of $A\\times B$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F7",
            "step 1.1",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "For any finite number $r$ of cross pairs, expand $\\widetilde H_\\mu^{\\otimes r}-\\widetilde W_\\mu^{\\otimes r}$ by telescoping; each summand contains one factor $\\widetilde H_\\mu-\\widetilde W_\\mu$. Its support is disjoint from the corresponding open neighborhood of $A\\times B$ by [F8] and step 2.1, so it pairs to zero with derivative-kernel tests supported in $(A\\times B)^r$. Therefore every finite cross contraction is unchanged when the time-ordered kernel is replaced by $W_\\mu$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "F8",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "In the finite contraction formula for $T_{p+q}^H(\\underbrace{V,\\ldots,V}_{p\\text{ inputs}}, \\underbrace{U,\\ldots,U}_{q\\text{ inputs}})$, split $\\sum_{i<j}D_{\\mathsf H}^{ij}$ into contractions within the first $p$ slots, within the last $q$ slots, and between the two blocks. These constant-coefficient operators commute because they act on specified independent slots. The first two groups give $T_p^H(V,\\ldots,V)$ and $T_q^H(U,\\ldots,U)$; by step 3.1 the cross group is exactly the $\\star_{W_\\mu}$ contraction. Every remaining field variable comes from an original coefficient kernel, so within-block contractions retain support in $A$ or $B$. Hence $$ T_{p+q}^H(\\underbrace{V,\\ldots,V}_{p\\text{ inputs}}, \\underbrace{U,\\ldots,U}_{q\\text{ inputs}})=T_p^H(V,\\ldots,V)\\star_{W_\\mu}T_q^H(U,\\ldots,U). $$ For $p=0$ or $q=0$, the empty block is $T_0^H=1$ and the same identity follows directly.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "In the degree-$n$ coefficient of $S_z^H(V+U)$, symmetry of $T_n^H$ groups terms with $p$ copies of $V$ and $q$ copies of $U$, $p+q=n$, with factor $\\binom{n}{p}$. Dividing by $n!$ gives $1/(p!q!)$. Substitution of step 4.1 therefore gives exactly the degree-$n$ coefficient of $S_z^H(V)\\star_{W_\\mu}S_z^H(U)$. Since $n$ was arbitrary, the causal-factorization identity holds coefficientwise, with no convergence in $z$.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F4",
            "step 4.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-6-1",
          "claim": "If $A,B$ are pairwise spacelike separated, step 1.1 gives $\\widetilde W_\\mu=\\widetilde W_\\mu^{\\mathsf T}$ on $A\\times B$. Swapping the two inputs in the finite $\\star_{W_\\mu}$ formula reverses each cross pairing and changes no within-input term, so $F\\star_{W_\\mu}G=G\\star_{W_\\mu}F$. Applying this coefficientwise to the formal series proves their commutation.",
          "step": "6.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 4.1 handles p=0 or q=0 with T_0^H=1; step 5.1 includes n=0."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The coefficientwise identity in step 5.1 includes zero interaction inputs."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 4.1 handles a one-input block, and step 5.1 applies for n=1."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The absent-support cases are the empty-block cases in step 4.1."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 2.1 excludes the closed past cone, including its null boundary."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The proof uses finite contraction sums and finite partitions of unity, not a choice axiom."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The theorem is an implication, not a biconditional."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The theorem is an implication, not a biconditional."
        }
      ],
      "finite_checks": [
        {
