    "thm-qft-bounded-regulator-dyson-factorization": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "4.2",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-hilbert-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **real Hilbert space** is a real inner-product space $H$ ([[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]]) whose induced-length metric is complete in the sense of [[def-complete-metric-space]]: every Cauchy sequence in $H$ for the norm $\\|v\\|=\\sqrt{\\langle v,v\\rangle}$ ([[cor-inner-product-induces-a-norm]]) converges to a point of $H$. A **complex Hilbert space** is a complex inner-product space with the same completeness property, so that a Hilbert space is exactly a real or complex inner-product space that is a Banach space for its induced norm ([[def-banach-space]]).\n\n**The completion convention is the Cauchy-sequence one.**\n[[def-banach-space]] defines completeness by convergence of Cauchy sequences, and this page keeps that convention throughout. It is weaker than $\\sigma$-completeness — the assertion that every decreasing sequence of nonempty closed subsets with diameters tending to zero has a nonempty intersection. The two agree in ZFC, but Blackadar, Farah and Karagila note that the closest-point theorem on a $\\sigma$-complete inner-product space is provable in ZF, whereas the Cauchy-complete form used below consumes the Axiom of Countable Choice; nothing here silently imports the stronger notion.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "4.2",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-space-of-bounded-linear-operators",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X$ and $Y$ be normed spaces over the same scalar field. Write\n\n$$\\mathcal B(X,Y) := \\{T:X\\to Y : T \\text{ is a bounded linear operator}\\}.$$\n\nThis is a subspace of the vector space $\\mathcal L(X,Y)$ of\n[[def-vector-space-of-linear-maps]], with the same pointwise operations:\n\n$$(S+T)(x) := Sx+Tx,\\qquad (\\lambda T)(x) := \\lambda\\,Tx.$$\n\nIf $X=Y$, write\n\n$$\\mathcal B(X) := \\mathcal B(X,X).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "4.2",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-operator-norm",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X$ and $Y$ be normed spaces over the same scalar field, and let\n$T:X\\to Y$ be bounded in the sense of [[def-bounded-linear-operator]]. The\n**operator norm** of $T$ is\n\n$$\\|T\\| := \\sup\\{\\|Tx\\| : x \\in X,\\ \\|x\\| \\le 1\\}.$$\n\nThe set on the right contains $0$ and every bound $C$ for $T$ bounds it\nabove. Thus its finite real supremum exists by the proved least-upper-bound\nproperty of the library's real field ([[cor-cauchy-reals-lub-complete]]).\nIn particular every number strictly below the supremum is exceeded by a\nmember of this set; otherwise it would be a smaller upper bound. No choice\nprinciple is used for this assertion.\n\nThe same number is the least bound of $T$:\n\n$$\\|T\\| = \\inf\\{C \\ge 0 : \\|Tx\\| \\le C\\|x\\| \\text{ for all } x \\in X\\}.$$\n\nIf $X \\ne \\{0\\}$, positive homogeneity also gives\n\n$$\\|T\\| = \\sup\\{\\|Tx\\| : \\|x\\| = 1\\}.$$\n\nWhen $X = \\{0\\}$, the unit sphere is empty and the unit-ball supremum is $0$,\nso $\\|T\\| = 0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "4.2",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-hilbert-space-adjoint",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $H$ and $K$ be real or complex Hilbert spaces and let $T\\in\\mathcal B(H,K)$ be a bounded linear operator ([[def-bounded-linear-operator]], [[def-space-of-bounded-linear-operators]]). For fixed $y\\in K$ the map\n\n$$x\\longmapsto\\langle Tx,y\\rangle_K$$\n\nis a bounded linear functional on $H$: it is linear in $x$ because $T$ is linear and the pairing is linear in its first argument, and $|\\langle Tx,y\\rangle_K|\\le\\|Tx\\|\\,\\|y\\|\\le\\|T\\|\\,\\|x\\|\\,\\|y\\|$ by Cauchy–Schwarz and the operator-norm inequality ([[thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space]], [[def-operator-norm]]). By Riesz representation ([[thm-riesz-representation-for-hilbert-space]]) there is therefore a unique vector $T^*y\\in H$ with\n\n$$\\langle Tx,y\\rangle_K=\\langle x,T^*y\\rangle_H\\qquad\\text{for every }x\\in H .$$\n\nThe **Hilbert adjoint** of $T$ is the map\n\n$$T^*:K\\longrightarrow H,\\qquad y\\longmapsto T^*y .$$\n\nIt is the unique map $K\\to H$ satisfying the displayed identity, since two such maps have $\\langle x,(T^*_1-T^*_2)y\\rangle=0$ for all $x$ and hence $(T^*_1-T^*_2)y=0$ by positive definiteness.\n\n**The dictionary with the Banach transpose.** Write $R_H:H\\to H^*$ and $R_K:K\\to K^*$ for the Riesz maps $R_H(x)=\\langle\\,\\cdot\\,,x\\rangle_H$ and $R_K(y)=\\langle\\,\\cdot\\,,y\\rangle_K$, and let $T'\\in\\mathcal B(K^*,H^*)$ be the transpose of $T$, $(T'g)(x)=g(Tx)$ ([[def-transpose-of-a-bounded-operator]]). Then for all $y\\in K$ and $x\\in H$,\n\n$$\\bigl(R_H(T^*y)\\bigr)(x)=\\langle x,T^*y\\rangle_H=\\langle Tx,y\\rangle_K=(R_Ky)(Tx)=\\bigl(T'(R_Ky)\\bigr)(x),$$\n\nso $R_H\\,T^*=T'\\,R_K$. The Hilbert adjoint is thus the Banach transpose conjugated by the Riesz identifications of $H$ and $K$ with their duals; it is a different operator from $T'$ whenever the Riesz maps are conjugate-linear.",
          "uses": [
            "4.2",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-extreme-value-metric",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,d)$ be a nonempty compact metric space ([[def-metric-compactness]],\n[[def-metric-space]]) and let $f : X \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be continuous\n([[def-metric-continuity]]), $\\mathbb{R}$ carrying its usual metric\n$d_{\\mathbb{R}}(s,t) = |s-t|$ ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]]). Then the\nimage $f[X]$ is bounded above and below ([[def-bounded-set]]), and it has a\nmaximum and a minimum ([[def-max-min]]): there are points\n$x_{\\max}, x_{\\min} \\in X$ with\n\n$$f(x_{\\min}) \\;\\le\\; f(x) \\;\\le\\; f(x_{\\max}) \\qquad \\text{for every } x \\in X,$$\n\nand then $f(x_{\\max}) = \\sup f[X]$ and $f(x_{\\min}) = \\inf f[X]$\n([[def-complete-ordered-field]], [[def-infimum]]).\n\nNonemptiness of $X$ is a hypothesis and not an oversight: for $X = \\emptyset$ the\nimage is empty and has neither a supremum nor a maximum. No choice principle is\nused.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-complex-exponential",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\iota_{\\mathbb R}\\colon\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb R$ be the canonical-natural map\nof [[def-canonical-natural]], and let $j\\colon\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb C$ be the real\nembedding $j(x)=x+i0$ from [[def-complex-numbers-and-arithmetic]]. For every\n$n\\in\\mathbb N$, the factorial $n!$ is nonzero by\n[[def-factorial-and-falling-factorial]], so\n$\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!)>0$ by [[lem-of-naturals-positive]] and consequently\n$j(\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!))\\ne0$ in the complex field\n[[thm-complex-numbers-form-a-field]].\n\nFor $z\\in\\mathbb C$, define\n$$ \\exp z:=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{z^n}{j(\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!))}. $$\nwhenever this complex series converges. Inside a complex expression we\nabbreviate the embedded denominator $j(\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!))$ by $n!$, so the\nsame definition may be written $\\exp z=\\sum z^n/n!$ without identifying a\nnatural number with a complex number. Powers and series are those of\n[[def-complex-integer-powers]] and\n[[def-complex-series-power-series-and-absolute-convergence]]. The convergence\nfor every $z\\in\\mathbb C$ is discharged by\n[[lem-complex-exponential-series-converges-everywhere]].",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-complex-exponential-series-converges-everywhere",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every $z\\in\\mathbb C$, the series $\\sum z^n/n!$ converges absolutely.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-continuous-on-a-rectangle-is-riemann-integrable",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Every continuous real function on a closed nondegenerate rectangle $Q\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$, $m\\ge1$, is Riemann integrable.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-ftc-second-part",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a < b$ be reals, let $G : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be differentiable at every\npoint of $[a,b]$ as a function on $[a,b]$ ([[def-derivative]]; at $a$ and $b$\nthis is the one-sided derivative), let $f := G'$, and suppose $f$ is integrable\non $[a,b]$ ([[def-darboux-integral]]). Then\n\n$$\\int_a^b f \\;=\\; G(b) - G(a) .$$\n\n**Both hypotheses are needed and neither is removable.** A function may be\ndifferentiable everywhere with $G'$ not integrable — then the left-hand side does\nnot exist (an everywhere differentiable function with unbounded derivative) — and an\nintegrable $f$ need not be the derivative of anything\n(the sign function); both witnesses are on the\ncompanion page.\n\n**No continuity of $f$ is assumed**, which is what makes this the working form:\nthe theorem evaluates $\\int_a^b f$ for every integrable derivative, not only for\ncontinuous integrands.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $c \\in A$ be a limit point of $A$\n([[def-limit-point-r]]), let $f, g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be differentiable at $c$\n([[def-derivative]]) and let $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then:\n\n1. $f + g$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(f+g)'(c) = f'(c) + g'(c)$;\n2. $\\alpha f$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(\\alpha f)'(c) = \\alpha f'(c)$;\n3. $fg$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(fg)'(c) = f'(c)g(c) + f(c)g'(c)$;\n4. if $g(c) \\ne 0$ then, writing $A_0 := \\{\\, x \\in A : g(x) \\ne 0 \\,\\}$, the\n   point $c$ lies in $A_0$ and is a limit point of $A_0$, the quotient\n   $(f/g)|_{A_0} : A_0 \\to \\mathbb{R}$, $x \\mapsto f(x)/g(x)$, is differentiable\n   at $c$ as a function on $A_0$, and\n   $$\\bigl((f/g)|_{A_0}\\bigr)'(c) \\;=\\; \\frac{f'(c)\\,g(c) - f(c)\\,g'(c)}{g(c)^{2}} .$$\n\nEach claim asserts two things: that the derivative on the left exists, and that\nit has the stated value. Both are proved.\n\n**Why claim 4 is stated on $A_0$.** The function $f/g$ is not defined where $g$\nvanishes, and $g$ may vanish at points of $A$ far from $c$; restricting to $A_0$\nis forced. That the restriction still has $c$ as a limit point, so that a\nderivative there means anything at all, is not free either, and it is the last\nclaim of [[lem-sign-preservation-near-a-limit]] applied to $g$. The hypothesis\nis $g(c) \\ne 0$, not \"$g$ vanishes nowhere\".\n\n**Everything is proved through\n[[thm-caratheodory-characterisation]].** No difference quotient is estimated and\nno limit theorem beyond continuity is used, so no choice principle is spent.\nThe four identities are four algebraic rearrangements of an increment, each\nfollowed by a reading of [[thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions]].",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-heine-cantor-metric",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,d_X)$ be a compact metric space ([[def-metric-compactness]]), let\n$(Y,d_Y)$ be any metric space ([[def-metric-space]]) and let $f : X \\to Y$ be\ncontinuous ([[def-metric-continuity]]). Then $f$ is uniformly continuous\n([[def-metric-uniform-continuity]]).\n\nNo choice principle is used: the cover built below is cut out by a property, and\nthe Lebesgue number lemma it is fed to is itself choice free\n([[thm-lebesgue-number-lemma]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "If $a=b$, every integral is zero, $U_0=I$, and $U_n=0$ for $n\\ge1$; all evolution and unitarity claims follow, while no $c<d$ lies in $I$, so the separated-support clause has no instance. Assume henceforth $a<b$. Suppose $U_n(r,s)\\psi$ is continuous in $r$. For fixed $r_0$, $$ \\|V(r)U_n(r,s)\\psi-V(r_0)U_n(r_0,s)\\psi\\| \\le M\\|U_n(r,s)\\psi-U_n(r_0,s)\\psi\\| +\\|(V(r)-V(r_0))U_n(r_0,s)\\psi\\|, $$ which tends to zero by strong continuity of $V$. Thus the recursive integrand is continuous. Apply [F7] to the compact interval $I=[a,b]$ with metric $|r-r'|$\nand the Hilbert space with norm metric $\\|u-v\\|$. The preceding estimate\nproves continuity of $r\\mapsto V(r)U_n(r,s)\\psi$ for each fixed $s,\\psi$,\nso [F7] gives its uniform continuity. Its vector-valued Riemann integral\ntherefore exists: this uniform continuity makes the tagged sums for the explicit equal-mesh partitions Cauchy; completeness of $\\mathcal H$ gives their limit. Uniform continuity also bounds the difference between sums on any two sufficiently fine tagged partitions by a quantity tending to zero, so this limit is independent of the partitions and tags. The tagged-sum triangle inequality, followed by the scalar Riemann-sum limit from [F5], gives $\\|\\int f\\|\\le\\int\\|f\\|$. The integral as a function of its upper endpoint is continuous, since its increment has norm at most the interval length times the supremum of the continuous integrand; this supremum is finite by [F3]. This proves strong continuity of $U_{n+1}(\\cdot,s)\\psi$ and closes the induction.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A2",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "F7",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The identity operator has norm at most one, including when $\\mathcal H=\\{0\\}$. The integral estimate then gives inductively $$ \\|U_{n+1}(t,s)\\| \\le\\int_{\\min(s,t)}^{\\max(s,t)}M\\frac{M^n|r-s|^n}{n!}\\,dr =\\frac{M^{n+1}|t-s|^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}. $$ This proves the factorial bound for both orientations of the integral.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F1",
            "A2",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The bound in step 2.1 makes $\\sum z^nU_n(t,s)$ absolutely and uniformly convergent in operator norm on $I^2$, with total norm at most $\\sum_{n\\ge0}(|z|M(b-a))^n/n!=e^{|z|M(b-a)}$ by [F4]. The recursion and this uniform bound permit termwise integration on each vector: the norm of the integral of a uniform tail is at most $(b-a)$ times its supremum norm, which tends to zero. This gives the displayed integral equation.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A2",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "If $X(t,s)$ and $Y(t,s)$ are two strongly continuous solutions, then for fixed $\\psi$, $D(t)=\\|(X(t,s)-Y(t,s))\\psi\\|$ is bounded on $I$ by [F3] and is Riemann integrable by [F5]. It satisfies $D(t)\\le |z|M\\int_{\\min(s,t)}^{\\max(s,t)}D(r)\\,dr$. Repeated substitution gives an $n$-fold integral over the ordered simplex between $s$ and $t$; induction using [F5] and the polynomial derivative rule [F6] evaluates its volume as $|t-s|^n/n!$, so $$ D(t)\\le C_\\psi\\frac{(|z|M|t-s|)^n}{n!} $$ with $C_\\psi=\\sup_{r\\in I}D(r)<\\infty$. The right side tends to zero, so the solution is unique. To prove composition, split the equation for $U_z(t,s)$ at $r$: for $t\\ge r$, it reads $U_z(t,s)=U_z(r,s)-iz\\int_r^tV(q)U_z(q,s)\\,dq$. Multiplying the equation for $U_z(t,r)$ on the right by $U_z(r,s)$ shows $U_z(t,r)U_z(r,s)$ solves this same initial-value equation and has the same value at $t=r$. The uniqueness estimate just proved, with initial time $r$, gives equality; the reversed orientation proves it for $t<r$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 3.1",
            "A2",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-2",
          "claim": "For a continuous vector-valued integrand, the average over a signed interval of length $h$ converges in norm to its value at the base point: the error is bounded by the supremum of its variation on that interval. Taking difference quotients in the integral equation therefore gives $\\partial_tU_z(t,s)\\psi=-izV(t)U_z(t,s)\\psi$. If $u(t)=U_z(t,s)\\psi$, then $$ \\frac{\\|u(t+h)\\|^2-\\|u(t)\\|^2}{h} =2\\operatorname{Re}\\left\\langle u(t),\\frac{u(t+h)-u(t)}h\\right\\rangle +\\frac{\\|u(t+h)-u(t)\\|^2}{h}. $$ The second term tends to zero and the first tends to $2\\operatorname{Re}\\langle u(t),-izV(t)u(t)\\rangle=0$ for real $z$, because self-adjointness makes $\\langle u,V(t)u\\rangle$ real. Thus $U_z(t,s)$ is an isometry.",
          "step": "4.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "A2",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "By the composition law in step 4.1, $U_z(t,s)U_z(s,t)=U_z(t,t)=I_{\\mathcal H}$ and $U_z(s,t)U_z(t,s)=U_z(s,s)=I_{\\mathcal H}$. Hence $U_z(t,s)$ is surjective. Step 4.2 shows that it is an isometry when $z$ is real. By the unitary convention stated above, $U_z(t,s)$ is unitary.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 4.1",
            "step 4.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-6-1",
          "claim": "Let $Y(t)=U_z^{V_1}(t,a)U_z^{V_2}(t,a)$. For each $\\psi$, $$ \\frac{Y(t+h)\\psi-Y(t)\\psi}{h} =\\frac{U_z^{V_1}(t+h,a)-U_z^{V_1}(t,a)}{h}U_z^{V_2}(t+h,a)\\psi +U_z^{V_1}(t,a)\\frac{U_z^{V_2}(t+h,a)-U_z^{V_2}(t,a)}{h}\\psi. $$ Strong continuity, the local operator bounds, and the differential equations from step 4.2 make the right side converge to the product-rule derivative. Before the later support interval, $U_z^{V_1}(t,a)=I$; after the earlier support interval, $U_z^{V_2}(t,a)$ is constant. Thus on the earlier interval, the gap, and the later interval, this derivative is $\\partial_tY=-iz(V_1(t)+V_2(t))Y$, and $Y(a)=I$. Integrating across the three regions and using continuity at their endpoints shows that $Y$ solves the integral equation for $V_1+V_2$. Uniqueness from step 4.1 yields the stated factorization at $b$. Both sides have operator-norm-convergent power series for every $z$. Their equality for all real $z$ forces equality of coefficients: if a first coefficient differed, divide the difference by its least power of $z$ and let real $z\\to0$, contradicting the equality.",
          "step": "6.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A2",
            "A3",
            "F4",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "step 4.1",
            "step 4.2",
            "algebra",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.1 treats a=b with U_0=I and U_n=0 for n≥1."
