          "evidence": "Steps 1.1 and 6.1 include interval endpoints and the ordered supports c<d."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "A1 states the Countable Choice strength for the Hilbert adjoint; step 1.1 constructs vector integrals from explicit equal partitions."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Statement of Bounded regulated Dyson convergence and temporal factorization: Assume Countable Choice. Let $\\mathcal H$ be a complex Hilbert space ([[def-hilbert-space]]), $I=[a,b]$ with $a\\le b$, and $V:I\\to\\mathcal B(\\mathcal H)$ a strongly continuous family of self-adjoint bounded operators (self-adjoint means $V(t)=V(t)^*$, using the Hilbert adjoint of [[def-hilbert-space-adjoint]]) with $M:=\\sup_{t\\in I}\\|V(t)\\|<\\infty$. Strong continuity means that $t\\mapsto V(t)\\psi$ is norm-continuous for every $\\psi\\in\\mathcal H$. Define $U_0(t,s)=I_{\\mathcal H}$ and recursively $$ U_{n+1}(t,s)\\psi=-i\\int_s^t V(r)U_n(r,s)\\psi\\,dr,\\qquad a\\le s,t\\le b, $$ where the integral is the vector-valued Riemann integral and is oriented when $t<s$. Then $$ \\|U_0(t,s)\\|\\le1,\\qquad \\|U_n(t,s)\\|\\le \\frac{M^n| The forward iff axis adds no separately stated converse hypothesis here; the recorded claim is the displayed construction, identity, implication or scope qualification, not a characterization by a new necessary-and-sufficient condition."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Statement of Bounded regulated Dyson convergence and temporal factorization: Assume Countable Choice. Let $\\mathcal H$ be a complex Hilbert space ([[def-hilbert-space]]), $I=[a,b]$ with $a\\le b$, and $V:I\\to\\mathcal B(\\mathcal H)$ a strongly continuous family of self-adjoint bounded operators (self-adjoint means $V(t)=V(t)^*$, using the Hilbert adjoint of [[def-hilbert-space-adjoint]]) with $M:=\\sup_{t\\in I}\\|V(t)\\|<\\infty$. Strong continuity means that $t\\mapsto V(t)\\psi$ is norm-continuous for every $\\psi\\in\\mathcal H$. Define $U_0(t,s)=I_{\\mathcal H}$ and recursively $$ U_{n+1}(t,s)\\psi=-i\\int_s^t V(r)U_n(r,s)\\psi\\,dr,\\qquad a\\le s,t\\le b, $$ where the integral is the vector-valued Riemann integral and is oriented when $t<s$. Then $$ \\|U_0(t,s)\\|\\le1,\\qquad \\|U_n(t,s)\\|\\le \\frac{M^n| The reverse iff axis adds no separately stated converse hypothesis here; the recorded claim is the displayed construction, identity, implication or scope qualification, not a characterization by a new necessary-and-sufficient condition."
        }
      ],
      "finite_checks": [
        {
          "check": "For n=1, evaluate the factorial bound in the first nontrivial Dyson order.",
