    "thm-qft-diagonal-delta-distribution": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, with $n\\ge1$. A complex-valued function is smooth if its real and imaginary parts are $C^k$ for every finite $k$, in the sense of [[def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables]]. Coordinate derivatives act on these two parts separately. We write $\\partial^\\alpha$ for the multi-index convention there, and $\\partial^0\\varphi=\\varphi$.\n\nThe **test-function space** is $\\mathcal D(\\Omega)=C_c^\\infty(\\Omega;\\mathbb C)$: its members are smooth functions whose support, the closure in $\\Omega$ of their nonzero set, is a compact subset of $\\Omega$. Equivalently, the zero extension to $\\mathbb R^n$ is smooth with compact support contained in $\\Omega$: compactness gives a neighborhood of every boundary point disjoint from the support, where that extension vanishes. Functions are actual functions, not equivalence classes.\n\nPointwise addition and scalar multiplication make this a complex vector space; the support of a sum lies in the union of the two compact supports. The zero function has empty support. If $\\Omega=\\varnothing$ there is just the empty function, which is the zero vector. The topology is specified in subsequent definitions. Distribution pairings throughout this page are complex bilinear, with no conjugation on test functions. One may restrict all constructions to real scalars.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-test-function-topology",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open. For the fixed-support spaces of [[def-fixed-support-test-function-frechet-space]], let $\\mathcal P$ be the set of all seminorms $q:\\mathcal D(\\Omega)\\to[0,\\infty)$ such that $q|_{\\mathcal D_K}$ is continuous for every compact $K\\subseteq\\Omega$. A seminorm means $q(av)=|a|q(v)$ and $q(v+w)\\le q(v)+q(w)$.\n\nThe **test-function topology** is the topology generated by translated finite intersections of sets $\\{\\varphi:q(\\varphi)<\\varepsilon\\}$, where $q\\in\\mathcal P$ and $\\varepsilon>0$. Equivalently it is the finest locally convex topology making every inclusion $\\mathcal D_K\\hookrightarrow\\mathcal D(\\Omega)$ continuous; the universal-property lemma below proves this equivalence and the topology assertions. This is the locally convex inductive-limit or **LF topology**, not the unrestricted final topology of arbitrary spaces.\n\nFor precision, a locally convex topology here is a vector topology generated by a family of seminorms, and boundedness means absorption by every zero-neighborhood: for each such neighborhood $U$ there is $t>0$ with $B\\subseteq tU$. Finite intersections may be empty, giving the whole space. When $\\Omega$ is empty there is only the zero vector and its unique topology. No convergence criterion for sequences is used in the definition; that criterion will be a theorem.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-distribution",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For open $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$, a **distribution** is a continuous complex-linear map $u:\\mathcal D(\\Omega)\\to\\mathbb C$, with the test-function topology of [[def-test-function-topology]]. Their vector space is denoted $\\mathcal D'(\\Omega)$. Write $\\langle u,\\varphi\\rangle=u(\\varphi)$; the pairing is linear in both arguments, with no conjugation.\n\nContinuity means continuity for that topology: for each $\\varepsilon>0$ the set $\\{|u(\\varphi)|<\\varepsilon\\}$ contains a zero-neighborhood. By linearity this suffices at every point. Sums and scalar multiples remain continuous and linear, so the indicated collection is a complex vector space. If $\\Omega=\\varnothing$, its test space is zero and its distribution space is zero as well. The finite-order estimate and the sequential criterion are established separately; neither substitutes for continuity in this definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $m\\ge1$, let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be open, and let $f:U\\to\\mathbb R$. A **multi-index** is $\\alpha=(\\alpha_0,\\ldots,\\alpha_{m-1})\\in\\mathbb N^m$. Put\n\n$$|\\alpha|:=\\sum_{i<m}\\alpha_i,\\qquad \\alpha!:=\\prod_{i<m}\\alpha_i!,\\qquad h^\\alpha:=\\prod_{i<m}h_i^{\\alpha_i}\\quad(h\\in\\mathbb R^m).$$\n\nHere $|\\alpha|$ and $\\alpha!$ use the natural-number sum and product of [[def-nat-finite-sum-and-product]], and $n!$ is the factorial of [[def-factorial-and-falling-factorial]]. By contrast, $h^\\alpha$ is the finite product in $\\mathbb R$ of [[def-finite-sum]], with the natural exponents interpreted by [[def-integer-power]]. For the zero multi-index $0$, set $D^0f:=f$. For nonzero $\\alpha$, write\n\n$$D^\\alpha f:=\\partial_0^{\\alpha_0}\\cdots\\partial_{m-1}^{\\alpha_{m-1}}f$$\n\nfor this displayed, canonical order whenever it exists. Coordinate partial derivatives have the meaning fixed in [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]].\n\nFor $k\\in\\mathbb N$, $f$ is of class $C^k$ on $U$ when, for every word $(i_1,\\ldots,i_r)$ of coordinate indices with $0\\le r\\le k$, the iterated derivative $\\partial_{i_r}\\cdots\\partial_{i_1}f$ exists and is continuous on $U$; the word of length $0$ denotes $f$. Thus this definition does **not** presuppose that differently ordered derivatives are equal. Equality of their values is a later theorem under these regularity hypotheses.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f:U\\to V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be totally differentiable at $a\\in U$ and let $g:V\\to\\mathbb R^p$ be totally differentiable at $f(a)$. Then $g\\circ f$ is totally differentiable at $a$ and\n\n$$D(g\\circ f)(a)=Dg(f(a))\\circ Df(a).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-continuous-on-a-rectangle-is-riemann-integrable",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Every continuous real function on a closed nondegenerate rectangle $Q\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$, $m\\ge1$, is Riemann integrable.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-test-function-cutoffs-and-euclidean-localization",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In ZF, for compact $K\\subseteq\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ with $\\Omega$ open, there is $\\chi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\Omega)$ with $0\\le\\chi\\le1$ and $\\chi=1$ on a neighborhood of $K$. Every open cover of $\\Omega$ admits an at most countable locally finite smooth partition of unity with compact supports, each support contained in some cover member. Subordination here asserts existence of such a member for each support; it does not select cover labels.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-support-of-a-distribution",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $u\\in\\mathcal D'(\\Omega)$, say $u$ **vanishes on** an open $V\\subseteq\\Omega$ if its restriction to $V$ is zero. Let $Z$ be the union of all such open sets. Locality in [[thm-distributions-form-a-sheaf]] shows that $u|_Z=0$, since these sets cover $Z$ and all its restrictions are zero. Thus $Z$ is the largest vanishing open set. The **support** is the relatively closed set $\\operatorname{supp}u=\\Omega\\setminus Z$.\n\nIn particular $\\operatorname{supp}u=\\varnothing$ if and only if $u=0$: emptiness gives $Z=\\Omega$ and vanishing there, while the zero distribution vanishes everywhere. If a test $\\varphi$ vanishes on a neighborhood of $\\operatorname{supp}u$, its compact support is contained in $Z$, so $u(\\varphi)=0$ by restriction. Consequently two tests agreeing on a neighborhood of $\\operatorname{supp}u$ have equal pairings with $u$, by applying this fact to their difference.\n\n**Compactly supported** means that $\\operatorname{supp}u$ is a compact subset of $\\Omega$. Relative closedness in an arbitrary open domain is not by itself compactness or closedness in the ambient Euclidean space. On the empty domain support is empty. The union and locality argument require no selection of vanishing neighborhoods and no choice axiom.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Define $d_X:X\\to X\\times X$ by $d_X(x)=(x,x)$. For $\\Psi\\in\\mathcal D(X\\times X)$, the diagonal restriction $x\\mapsto\\Psi(d_X(x))$ has first derivatives $\\partial_{x_j}\\Psi(x,x)+\\partial_{y_j}\\Psi(x,x)$ by [F2]. Iterating this formula on the smooth partial derivatives of $\\Psi$ shows the restriction has continuous derivatives of every order, so it is smooth. Its support is contained in the first-coordinate projection of $\\operatorname{supp}\\Psi$, which is compact as a continuous image of a compact set. Choose a compact rectangle containing that projection; [F3] makes the displayed integral well defined.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The assignment is complex-linear. Fix a nonempty compact $K\\subseteq X\\times X$ and a compact rectangle $R\\subseteq X$ containing its first-coordinate projection. For every $\\Psi$ supported in $K$, $$ |\\langle\\delta_{\\Delta_X},\\Psi\\rangle| \\le \\operatorname{vol}(R)\\sup_{(x,y)\\in K}|\\Psi(x,y)|. $$ Thus the seminorm $q(\\Psi)=|\\langle\\delta_{\\Delta_X},\\Psi\\rangle|$ is continuous on every fixed-support test space. By the LF topology definition in [F1], such seminorms are continuous on $\\mathcal D(X\\times X)$. Therefore $\\delta_{\\Delta_X}\\in\\mathcal D'(X\\times X)$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "If an open set $U\\subseteq X\\times X$ is disjoint from $\\Delta_X$, then every test supported in $U$ vanishes at every $(x,x)$. Its pairing with $\\delta_{\\Delta_X}$ is zero, so [F5] gives $\\operatorname{supp}\\delta_{\\Delta_X}\\subseteq\\Delta_X$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Fix $(x_0,x_0)\\in\\Delta_X$ and any open neighborhood $U$ of this point. There is a ball $B$ about $x_0$ with $\\overline B\\times\\overline B\\subseteq U$. By [F4] choose $h\\in\\mathcal D(X)$ with $h\\ge0$, $h(x_0)=1$, and support in $B$. The product test $\\Psi(x,y)=h(x)h(y)$ is supported in $U$, and $\\langle\\delta_{\\Delta_X},\\Psi\\rangle=\\int_X h(x)^2\\,d^4x>0$, since continuity makes $h$ positive on a ball about $x_0$. Thus $\\delta_{\\Delta_X}$ does not vanish on any neighborhood of $(x_0,x_0)$, so $(x_0,x_0)\\in\\operatorname{supp}\\delta_{\\Delta_X}$ by [F5]. Since the point was arbitrary, $\\Delta_X\\subseteq\\operatorname{supp}\\delta_{\\Delta_X}$. Together with step 3.1 this gives equality.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "For every $\\Psi$, the diagonal restrictions of $\\Psi(x,y)$ and $\\Psi(y,x)$ coincide, so the distribution is symmetric. The diagonal has empty interior: every product neighborhood of a point $(x,x)$ contains $(x,y)$ with $y\\ne x$. If a smooth function supported on the diagonal were nonzero at a point, continuity would make it nonzero on an open neighborhood, contradicting empty interior. Therefore every such smooth function is zero.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 4.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "finite_checks": [
        {
          "check": "For a nonnegative cutoff h supported near x0, test Psi(x,y)=h(x)h(y).",
          "result": "The diagonal pairing is integral h^2>0.",
          "verifies": "The support inclusion for each diagonal point in proof step 4.1."
        }
      ],
      "boundaries": [
