    "rem-qft-local-wick-diagonal-obligation": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qft-regular-polynomial-functional-algebra",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $d\\ge1$, $X=\\mathbb R^d$, and $n\\ge1$. Write\n$\\mathcal D(X^n)=C_c^\\infty(X^n;\\mathbb C)$ for the complex test-function\nspace of smooth compactly supported functions\n([[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]]), with its LF topology\n([[def-test-function-topology]]). The convergence criterion used below is\nthat convergent test sequences have one common compact support and converge\nuniformly with every derivative\n([[thm-sequential-convergence-in-test-function-space]]). Here $C^\\infty$\nmeans every coordinate derivative exists and is continuous\n([[def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables]]). All integrals are\ncompact Riemann integrals: each integrand is continuous with compact support,\nso restrict it to a compact rectangle and apply\n[[thm-continuous-on-a-rectangle-is-riemann-integrable]]. Let\n$\\mathcal D_{\\mathrm{sym}}(X^n)$ be the subspace of kernels invariant under\nevery permutation of their $n$ $X$-variables. Fields are real smooth\nconfigurations $\\phi\\in C^\\infty(X;\\mathbb R)$. For an integer $N\\ge0$\nand a finite family\n$f_0\\in\\mathbb C$, $f_n\\in\\mathcal D_{\\mathrm{sym}}(X^n)$ for\n$1\\le n\\le N$, define\n$$ F_f(\\phi)=f_0+\\sum_{n=1}^{N}\\int_{X^n}f_n(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)\\prod_{j=1}^{n}\\phi(x_j)\\,dx_1\\cdots dx_n. $$\nThe pairing is complex-bilinear, with no conjugation on the kernel or field.\nAn element of $\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}(X)$ is this finite symmetric\ncoefficient family, evaluated on fields by $F_f$. Pointwise sums, scalar\nmultiples, and products again have finite symmetric compactly supported\ncoefficient families: for products, expand the two finite sums and\nsymmetrize each tensor product $f_m\\otimes g_n$. Thus these elements form\nan algebra; the product-integral identity follows from Riemann Fubini on a\nrectangle containing the compact supports\n([[thm-riemann-fubini-on-product-rectangles]]).\n\nFor $k=0$, set $F_f^{(0)}(\\phi)=F_f(\\phi)$. For $1\\le k\\le N$,\nthe $k$-th functional derivative kernel assigned to this coefficient\nfamily is\n$$ F_f^{(k)}(\\phi)(x_1,\\ldots,x_k)=\\sum_{n=k}^{N}\\frac{n!}{(n-k)!}\\int_{X^{n-k}}f_n(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)\\prod_{j=k+1}^{n}\\phi(x_j)\\,dx_{k+1}\\cdots dx_n. $$\nFor $k>N$ it is zero. Each displayed kernel is smooth, symmetric and compactly supported in its $k$ remaining variables: compactness follows by projection of the finite union of coefficient-kernel supports, and derivatives pass through the finite-compact integrals by [[thm-differentiation-under-the-integral-sign-on-a-compact-rectangle]].\nIn the term $n=k$, the empty integral and empty product mean simply\n$f_k(x_1,\\ldots,x_k)$.\n\nThe scope is exactly smooth compact coefficient kernels. A local functional\nwhose higher derivative kernels are distributions on a thin diagonal is not\nincluded merely because its field expression is polynomial.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-qft-tensor-test-function-density",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $d,n\\ge1$, $X=\\mathbb R^d$, and give\n$\\mathcal D(X^n)=C_c^\\infty(X^n;\\mathbb C)$ its LF test-function topology.\nThe finite linear span of elementary product tests\n$$ (x_1,\\ldots,x_n)\\longmapsto\\prod_{j=1}^{n}g_j(x_j),\\qquad g_j\\in\\mathcal D(X), $$\nis dense. More precisely, for each $\\Phi\\in\\mathcal D(X^n)$, there is a\nsequence of such finite sums whose supports lie in one fixed compact product\nand whose derivatives of every multi-index converge uniformly to those of\n$\\Phi$. Symmetrized finite sums are dense in\n$\\mathcal D_{\\mathrm{sym}}(X^n)$. Consequently the symmetric-kernel\nrepresentation in [[def-qft-regular-polynomial-functional-algebra]] is\nunique: if a finite symmetric-kernel polynomial vanishes on every real smooth\nfield, every coefficient kernel vanishes.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-qft-diagonal-delta-distribution",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X=\\mathbb R^4$ and let $\\Delta_X=\\{(x,y)\\in X\\times X:x=y\\}$.\nFor $\\Psi\\in\\mathcal D(X\\times X)$, define\n$$ \\langle\\delta_{\\Delta_X},\\Psi\\rangle =\\int_X\\Psi(x,x)\\,d^4x. $$\nThen $\\delta_{\\Delta_X}\\in\\mathcal D'(X\\times X)$ is a continuous\ncomplex-linear distribution with support exactly $\\Delta_X$, and it is\nsymmetric under exchanging $x$ and $y$. No nonzero smooth function on\n$X\\times X$ is supported on $\\Delta_X$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open, with $n\\ge1$. A complex-valued function is smooth if its real and imaginary parts are $C^k$ for every finite $k$, in the sense of [[def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables]]. Coordinate derivatives act on these two parts separately. We write $\\partial^\\alpha$ for the multi-index convention there, and $\\partial^0\\varphi=\\varphi$.\n\nThe **test-function space** is $\\mathcal D(\\Omega)=C_c^\\infty(\\Omega;\\mathbb C)$: its members are smooth functions whose support, the closure in $\\Omega$ of their nonzero set, is a compact subset of $\\Omega$. Equivalently, the zero extension to $\\mathbb R^n$ is smooth with compact support contained in $\\Omega$: compactness gives a neighborhood of every boundary point disjoint from the support, where that extension vanishes. Functions are actual functions, not equivalence classes.\n\nPointwise addition and scalar multiplication make this a complex vector space; the support of a sum lies in the union of the two compact supports. The zero function has empty support. If $\\Omega=\\varnothing$ there is just the empty function, which is the zero vector. The topology is specified in subsequent definitions. Distribution pairings throughout this page are complex bilinear, with no conjugation on test functions. One may restrict all constructions to real scalars.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-test-function-cutoffs-and-euclidean-localization",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In ZF, for compact $K\\subseteq\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ with $\\Omega$ open, there is $\\chi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\Omega)$ with $0\\le\\chi\\le1$ and $\\chi=1$ on a neighborhood of $K$. Every open cover of $\\Omega$ admits an at most countable locally finite smooth partition of unity with compact supports, each support contained in some cover member. Subordination here asserts existence of such a member for each support; it does not select cover labels.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-support-of-a-distribution",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $u\\in\\mathcal D'(\\Omega)$, say $u$ **vanishes on** an open $V\\subseteq\\Omega$ if its restriction to $V$ is zero. Let $Z$ be the union of all such open sets. Locality in [[thm-distributions-form-a-sheaf]] shows that $u|_Z=0$, since these sets cover $Z$ and all its restrictions are zero. Thus $Z$ is the largest vanishing open set. The **support** is the relatively closed set $\\operatorname{supp}u=\\Omega\\setminus Z$.\n\nIn particular $\\operatorname{supp}u=\\varnothing$ if and only if $u=0$: emptiness gives $Z=\\Omega$ and vanishing there, while the zero distribution vanishes everywhere. If a test $\\varphi$ vanishes on a neighborhood of $\\operatorname{supp}u$, its compact support is contained in $Z$, so $u(\\varphi)=0$ by restriction. Consequently two tests agreeing on a neighborhood of $\\operatorname{supp}u$ have equal pairings with $u$, by applying this fact to their difference.\n\n**Compactly supported** means that $\\operatorname{supp}u$ is a compact subset of $\\Omega$. Relative closedness in an arbitrary open domain is not by itself compactness or closedness in the ambient Euclidean space. On the empty domain support is empty. The union and locality argument require no selection of vanishing neighborhoods and no choice axiom.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-test-function-topology",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open. For the fixed-support spaces of [[def-fixed-support-test-function-frechet-space]], let $\\mathcal P$ be the set of all seminorms $q:\\mathcal D(\\Omega)\\to[0,\\infty)$ such that $q|_{\\mathcal D_K}$ is continuous for every compact $K\\subseteq\\Omega$. A seminorm means $q(av)=|a|q(v)$ and $q(v+w)\\le q(v)+q(w)$.\n\nThe **test-function topology** is the topology generated by translated finite intersections of sets $\\{\\varphi:q(\\varphi)<\\varepsilon\\}$, where $q\\in\\mathcal P$ and $\\varepsilon>0$. Equivalently it is the finest locally convex topology making every inclusion $\\mathcal D_K\\hookrightarrow\\mathcal D(\\Omega)$ continuous; the universal-property lemma below proves this equivalence and the topology assertions. This is the locally convex inductive-limit or **LF topology**, not the unrestricted final topology of arbitrary spaces.\n\nFor precision, a locally convex topology here is a vector topology generated by a family of seminorms, and boundedness means absorption by every zero-neighborhood: for each such neighborhood $U$ there is $t>0$ with $B\\subseteq tU$. Finite intersections may be empty, giving the whole space. When $\\Omega$ is empty there is only the zero vector and its unique topology. No convergence criterion for sequences is used in the definition; that criterion will be a theorem.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-qft-regular-wick-star-associativity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X=\\mathbb R^d$, $d\\ge1$, and let $W\\in\\mathcal D'(X\\times X)$ be\nany continuous complex bidistribution. For a real formal parameter $\\eta$\nand $F,G\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}(X)$, define\n$$ F\\star_W G=\\sum_{k\\ge0}\\frac{\\eta^k}{k!}\\left\\langle W^{\\otimes k},F^{(k)}(\\phi)\\otimes G^{(k)}(\\phi)\\right\\rangle . $$\nThe pairing is over $k$ independent pairs of variables; $W^{\\otimes k}$\nis formed by iterated distributional tensor products, not by multiplying\ndistributions at coincident points. The sum is finite because the inputs are\npolynomial. It defines an element of\n$\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}(X)[\\eta]$, and $\\star_W$ is associative.\nIf $W$ is Hermitian in the sense\n$\\overline{\\langle W,\\overline g\\otimes\\overline f\\rangle}\n=\\langle W,f\\otimes g\\rangle$, then\n$(F\\star_W G)^*=G^*\\star_W F^*$, where $F^*(\\phi)=\\overline{F(\\phi)}$.\nFor $\\ell_f(\\phi)=\\int_X f(x)\\phi(x)\\,dx$,\n$$ [\\ell_f,\\ell_g]_{\\star_W}=\\eta\\bigl(W(f,g)-W(g,f)\\bigr), \\qquad W(f,g):=\\langle W,f\\otimes g\\rangle . $$",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "def-qft-regular-formal-time-ordering",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix $\\mu\\ge0$ and the $(-+++)$, shell, Fourier, and coordinate conventions\nof [[lem-qft-free-time-ordered-kernel]]. That current supplier states that\nits relative-coordinate tempered distribution $H_\\mu$ is symmetric, agrees\nwith the ordered Wightman kernels on strictly ordered time supports and on\nspacelike-separated supports, and has no prefactor $i$ in its definition.\nIts current carrier is a draft with a supplied conditional proof; the formula\nbelow uses that exact interface.\n\nFor open $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$, $\\mathcal D(\\Omega)$ is the space\nof smooth compactly supported complex tests with the LF topology, and\n$\\mathcal D'(\\Omega)$ is its continuous complex-linear dual, paired\nbilinearly with no conjugation\n([[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]], [[def-test-function-topology]],\n[[def-distribution]]). Schwartz space consists of smooth functions with all\npolynomially weighted derivatives bounded; $\\mathcal S'$ is its continuous\ncomplex-linear dual with bilinear pairing\n([[def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms]],\n[[def-schwartz-topology-and-convergence]], [[def-tempered-distribution]]).\n\nFor any relative-coordinate distribution $K\\in\\mathcal S'(\\mathbb R^4)$,\ndefine its translation-invariant two-point bidistribution\n$\\widetilde K\\in\\mathcal D'(\\mathbb R^4\\times\\mathbb R^4)$ by\n$$ \\langle\\widetilde K,\\Psi\\rangle =\\left\\langle K,\\ z\\longmapsto\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\Psi(y+z,y)\\,dy\\right\\rangle, \\qquad \\Psi\\in\\mathcal D(\\mathbb R^4\\times\\mathbb R^4). $$\nThe inner function is a test function: its support lies in the difference\nof the two compact coordinate projections of $\\operatorname{supp}\\Psi$;\neach derivative in $z$ is the Riemann integral of the corresponding\nfirst-variable derivative of $\\Psi$ over a compact rectangle containing\nthe fixed compact second-variable projection\n([[thm-continuous-on-a-rectangle-is-riemann-integrable]]).\nOn each fixed compact support space, every output derivative seminorm is\nbounded by the volume of a compact rectangle containing the second-variable\nprojection times the corresponding source derivative seminorm. The support\nof the output lies in the compact difference of the two projections. Thus\nthis map is continuous on each fixed-support test space, and hence on the LF\ntest topology [[def-test-function-topology]].\nThus the displayed formula defines a distribution without multiplying\nsingular distributions. Write $\\mathsf H_\\mu=\\widetilde H_\\mu$.\n\nFor $F\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}(\\mathbb R^4)$, define its support as the\nfinite union of the coordinate projections of the supports of its nonconstant\nsymmetric coefficient kernels. This is a compact set and is presentation\nindependent by [[lem-qft-tensor-test-function-density]]. For compact\n$A,B\\subset\\mathbb R^4$, write $A\\succcurlyeq_{\\mathrm{out}} B$ when\n$$ x-y\\notin C_-:=\\{(t,\\mathbf x)\\in\\mathbb R^4:t\\le-|\\mathbf x|\\} \\quad\\text{for every }x\\in A,\\ y\\in B. $$\nThis means the first support has no point in the closed causal past of the\nsecond, using the supplier's fixed inertial chart.\n\nFor $n\\ge2$ and $1\\le i<j\\le n$, let $D_{\\mathsf H}^{ij}$ contract one\nfunctional derivative in slots $i,j$ against $\\mathsf H_\\mu$, pairing\nindependent variables. Define\n$$ T_n^H(F_1,\\ldots,F_n)(\\phi) =\\left.m_n\\exp\\!\\left(\\eta\\sum_{1\\le i<j\\le n}D_{\\mathsf H}^{ij}\\right) \\prod_{i=1}^nF_i(\\phi_i)\\right|_{\\phi_1=\\cdots=\\phi_n=\\phi}, \\qquad T_0^H=1,\\quad T_1^H(F)=F. $$\nHere $m_n$ multiplies the remaining factors and then substitutes the same\nfield $\\phi$ in every slot; on coefficient kernels it symmetrizes their\nfinite tensor products. The parameter $\\eta$ is formal and fixed by\ncomplex conjugation. Each contraction removes two polynomial degrees, so the\nexponential is a finite sum on polynomial inputs. The dependencies\n[[thm-tensor-product-distributions-and-iterated-pairings]] and\n[[lem-distribution-pairing-with-smooth-parameter-families]] make every\ncontraction well defined and leave smooth compact kernels in the uncontracted\nvariables; no diagonal product occurs. Symmetry of $\\mathsf H_\\mu$ makes\n$T_n^H$ symmetric in its inputs.\n\nFor a regular compactly supported interaction $V$ and formal coupling $z$,\ndefine\n$$ S_z^H(V)=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{(iz/\\eta)^n}{n!}\\,T_n^H(V,\\ldots,V) \\quad\\text{with coefficients in }\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}(\\mathbb R^4)[\\eta,\\eta^{-1}]. $$\nThis is a coefficientwise formal series; the definition asserts no\nconvergence in $z$, no time-ordered product for singular local functionals,\nand no continuum interacting field.",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For real test directions $h,k$, the coefficient of $st$ in $L_g(1+s h+t k)$ is $12\\int g(x)h(x)k(x)\\,d^4x$, by expanding $(1+s h+t k)^4$. By [F3], this is exactly the pairing of $K_g$ with the product test $h\\otimes k$. Thus $K_g$ represents the second derivative on product tests.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For every nonempty fixed compact $K\\subseteq X\\times X$, choose a compact rectangle $R$ containing its first-coordinate projection. Then $$ |\\langle K_g,\\Psi\\rangle| \\le 12\\|g\\|_\\infty\\operatorname{vol}(R)\\|\\Psi\\|_\\infty \\qquad(\\operatorname{supp}\\Psi\\subseteq K), $$ so $K_g$ is continuous on each fixed-support test space and hence on the LF test topology by [F7]. It vanishes on every test supported off the diagonal, so its support is contained there by [F6]. Choose $\\rho\\in\\mathcal D(\\mathbb R^4)$ with $\\rho(0)=1$ using [F5], and set $\\Psi(x,y)=g(x)\\rho(x-y)$. This is a test function: its support has $x\\in\\operatorname{supp}g$ and $x-y\\in\\operatorname{supp}\\rho$, so both coordinate projections are compact. Then $$ \\langle K_g,\\Psi\\rangle=12\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}g(x)^2\\,d^4x>0, $$ because a nonzero continuous real function is bounded away from zero on a small ball. Thus $K_g\\ne0$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Suppose $L_g$ had a regular presentation. By [F1], its second derivative at $1$ would have a smooth compactly supported kernel. Its pairings with product tests agree with those of $K_g$ by step 1.1; complex linearity extends agreement to complex product tests, and density [F2] extends it to all tests. Hence the smooth kernel would equal $K_g$ as a distribution. Its support would be contained in the diagonal by step 2.1.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "The diagonal has empty interior. A continuous function supported there must vanish: any nonzero value would persist on an open neighborhood not contained in the diagonal. This contradicts $K_g\\ne0$ from step 2.1. Therefore $L_g$ has no regular presentation.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "By step 4.1, $L_g\\notin\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{reg}}$. Fact [F8] defines $\\star_W$ only on pairs of regular polynomial functionals, and [F9] defines each $T_n^H$ on regular polynomial inputs. Neither operation therefore applies to $L_g$. Any extension across the diagonal is a separate construction and is not proved here.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F8",
            "F9",
            "step 4.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
